window.harnessChunks["rows-5d48f35659fde866.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:08:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Auriga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga/tree/3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\">3e1be75 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue robot-manipulation goals through visual observation, task planning, multi-stage preview/execution, measured action feedback and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing object/robot geometry, failed or partial grasps, occlusion, movement slips, constrained arm trajectories and uncertain action completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next physical task action, observation, program, trajectory revision or recovery in light of returned evidence. - Decision owner: primary model-driven Codex actor (named Astra in optional verification documentation), with action authority mediated by the Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: action identity and current-observation checks, native motion planner, gripper guards, single action writer, program review broker, action ledger, wall/budget supervision and the physical simulator. - Closure path: Codex task/plan → robotobserve and robotaction preview → model selects execute → Harness.execute native worker → measured receipt/images/stop reason → model adjusts or terminates episode. - Boundary reachability: normal gateway creates the Codex session and Harness, registers robotaction and connects execution to native RoboDojo worker. The model/tool loop is shipped with documented flags, not merely a benchmark test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static robot safety gates do not select a new manipulation strategy; the actor model makes the task-level decisions even when a hard guard prevents unsafe execution. - Evidence: README execution loop, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct conflict-specific inter-S1 coordination and return is established in the supported robot actor organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dual-arm trajectory interference and concurrent program/native action requests are real actuator/resource hazards but not conflicts among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitrate independent agent commitments and return a coordination resolution; not evidenced. - Decision owner: one actor plans both arms; deterministic Harness serializes physical actions and rejects stale plans, rather than an S2 coordination owner regulating competing agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage-order dependencies, dual-arm path preview, active-writer exclusivity, plan revision, program broker approval and stop gates. - Closure path: native constraints and the primary actor can alter a manipulation task, but no separate inter-S1 interference-resolution result is fed into distinct operational agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: two robot arms are not independent goal-directed S1 cells; an optional evidence agent audits a claim but does not compete for physical task execution. - Evidence: unified actions, Harness action writer, program review. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-organization current-control authority over a population of operational S1 commitments is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: native action budget, simulator step accounting, active action conflicts, unknown stop confirmation, model transport failures and episode wall limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select systemic allocation/priority/commitment intervention across distinct operations; absent beyond the single robot episode&#39;s work plan. - Decision owner: primary actor owns task strategy; Harness enforces state/timeout/cost constraints; operator defines task/benchmark configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervision.waitturn, action gate, per-action budget, program source grants, GPU resource checks and status/feedback records. - Closure path: failures can stop one episode or prompt its actor to try another action, not a separate whole-current S3 decision that regulates multiple autonomous units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: counters, stop gates and request retry policy are enforcement; removing a hypothetical S3 model leaves them intact. - Evidence: runtime supervision, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: the primary robot actor&#39;s local claim that a selected grasp/lift/transfer/place/release or retreat phase achieved its expected visible result, as distinct from a native worker&#39;s narrower completed execution receipt. - Ordinary reporting path: Harness.execute returns a native action receipt and current measured observation to the primary actor, which can then report success on the strength of its own readings; a tool response alone does not prove object retention or task completion. - Complementary access path: the separately launched Sol reviewer is given the raw linked BEFORE/AFTER permitted RGB images, measured robot state, immutable action IDs/receipts and the actor&#39;s intended outcome as a hypothesis; it checks visual identity/relation independently and returns a structured met/notmet/uncertain verdict with gaps. - Independence boundary: CodexEvidenceAgent creates a fresh ephemeral thread, empty workspace and isolated home, disables memories/tools/hooks, and rejects observed helper tool activity; it is not forked from the acting Codex history and cannot issue motion commands. The actor chooses checkpoint and evidence, limiting scope but not transferring visual judgment to the actor. - Who acts on findings: the primary Astra actor receives visionreview results in its ordinary tool stream and is instructed to resolve unmet/uncertain evidence with fresh observations or changed recovery; the reviewer itself cannot execute or compel a trajectory and the operating loop retains physical action ownership. - Function: provide an independent, evidence-limited second opinion on a manipulation operation&#39;s claimed visible outcome and send discrepancies or uncertainty back to the acting agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic success interpretations of completed motion receipts, wrong target identity, occlusion, unsupported grasp/placement/release claims and partial execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge met, notmet or uncertain for a claimed phase by comparing independent before/after RGB, execution receipts and measured robot state, and specify evidence gaps/recovery feedback. - Decision owner: separate fresh gpt-6.1-sol evidence agent; primary Astra remains owner of the subsequent physical action and the operator decides whether to enable this configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic provenance and schema checks, isolated fresh home/thread, suppressed tools/memory, payload source hashes, shared model budget, exact model assertion and unsupported-positive veto in constrainanswer. - Closure path: actor selects meaningful checkpoint and invokes visionreview(evidencemode=&#39;operation&#39;) → fresh Sol independently examines linked images/receipts and issues bounded verdict and follow-up → tool response returns to the primary actor&#39;s Codex turn → actor instructed to resolve uncertainty or choose a changed recovery before later motions. The auditor never executes robot actions. - Boundary reachability: features.visualreviewmodel=&#39;gpt-6.1-sol&#39; is a documented supported config; the actual Workbench instantiates VisualReviewer(CodexEvidenceAgent(session)), the runtime tool handler dispatches it and returns the structured assessment to the ordinary actor context. Default null mode has no S3 helper; positive vector reflects the first-party enabled operating mode, not default execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh model provides the discretionary visual evidence judgment; deterministic validation limits unsupported assertions but cannot itself interpret ambiguous target retention/phase outcome. Actor authority to choose recovery is distinct from auditor judgment ownership. - Evidence: mode and return contract, Workbench construction, fresh agent isolation, review judgment, actor recovery instructions, tool handler. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: actor chooses evidence/request and need not obey a fixed compulsory verification phase sequence; this is a first-party supported optional independent second-opinion loop, not privileged simulator-ground-truth audit or guaranteed physical correction. No real provider/robot verification was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate externally prospective adaptation process with authorized whole-runtime capability-choice and return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel object geometries, repeated failed grasps, changing camera viewpoints, different robot scenes and future task transfer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: change organizational operating capability based on prospective environmental alternatives; not evidenced at the assessed one-episode recursion. - Decision owner: primary model adapts task actions in the current scene; developers/operators configure perception services, model and robot plugins. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adaptiveactioncheckpoint failure reminders, observation memory, trajectory cost previews, program synthesis and optional external perception. - Closure path: measured failures can prompt an immediate alternate grasp, viewpoint or trajectory within one task; no distinct environment/future analysis → approved organization-wide capability adaptation → feedback loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive recovery within one operating episode is S1 learning/action selection, not automatically S4 strategic adaptation. - Evidence: checkpoint helper, model-led recovery, unified actions/memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate organizational policy/identity arbitration with legitimate authoritative return is implemented inside the robot episode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robot safety boundaries, model permissions, execution modes, privileged task truth, tool grants and physical stop decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate identity-level commitments and revise binding policy for the whole; not shown beyond fixed configuration and task controls. - Decision owner: developer/operator specifies guardrails and modes; deterministic Harness enforces validation; primary actor decides ordinary manipulation steps within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actor observation redaction, action schema and state locks, program grants, model call budget and sandbox controls. - Closure path: individual actions are admitted/rejected against predefined rules, but no policy-level dispute → qualified authority → ultimate decision → new identity-governing operating rule is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may choose a risky trajectory and receive advisory data, but it does not own the system&#39;s ultimate identity or change invariant control policy. - Evidence: action gate, actor information boundary, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13/assessments/au/auriga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13\">ec6167d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T22:11:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dsh-superagent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype/tree/8c32a9dc5d72c033816c6a8110b37f40cf94fba4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8c32a9dc5d72c033816c6a8110b37f40cf94fba4\">8c32a9d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete a bounded repository work item through the worker&#39;s investigation, implementation and disposition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Heterogeneous change requests, repository conditions, incoming feedback and task blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose how to investigate and implement the accepted change, whether new inputs fit the mandate, and what outcome the ticket can report. - Decision owner: The bundle-wired ticket worker session. The main session controls acceptance and routing, and the operator or repository maintainer retains external authorization and review rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-ticket workspaces and persistent DSH sessions, ticket mandates, tool bindings and structured report schemas. - Closure path: Acceptance creates the ticket and mandate, the executor launches the worker session, and the worker&#39;s report updates the assignment and determines whether work continues, awaits input or ends. A bound PR can keep the ticket open through review and closure. - Boundary reachability: The standard runtime wires worker tools and prompts into the session that the ticket executor launches. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the worker&#39;s judgment leaves launch and state handling but removes implementation choices and the resulting work. - Evidence: src/core/evals.ts, src/app/runtime.ts, src/app/tools/executor.ts, src/core/assignments.ts, prompts/worker.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: The result is scoped to authorized work. Inferred candidates only dispatch automatically in auto mode. Hosted DSH model behavior and maintainer acceptance are external.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Resolve ambiguous or overlapping ownership of follow-up work among ticket workers sharing a source thread. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A follow-up can reach several tickets with different mandates. A misplaced or duplicate assignment can make multiple workers act on the same request or leave it with the wrong worker. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each affected worker judges whether to retain or hand back the follow-up. When ownership is released, the main session judges whether to attach it elsewhere, create distinct work or ignore it. - Decision owner: The shipped ticket worker roles and main orchestrator role, at their respective points in the hand-back loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Recorded trigger disposition and subscriptions, refusal of premature rerouting, requeue only after other ticket owners release the message, and an idempotent accept by trigger. - Closure path: A worker returns out-of-scope work with a reason. When no ticket still owns it, the next candidate carries the reasons and the main session can reroute. Attach supplies input to the selected ticket; accept creates a separate ticket. - Boundary reachability: Worker hand-back and main routing tools are registered by the runtime for active ticket and main sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime enforces the hand-back conditions. It does not choose whether a concrete follow-up fits a ticket&#39;s mandate or which ticket should receive released work. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent ticket workers with independent mandates and persistent sessions in the same deployment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Shared source threads can carry separate pieces of work and reach several ticket workers, with scope overlap or mistaken assignment of a new follow-up. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workers judge ownership and release misrouted input, and the main role can attach the released input to a better-fitting ticket using current ticket summaries. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The return reason becomes candidate context. The subsequent routing result changes which worker receives an actionable input and under which mandate it acts. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The return is tied to an identified ownership conflict across live units. The runtime blocks a second assignment while a ticket still owns the follow-up. - Evidence: docs/001-domain.md, docs/002-authorization.md, src/core/evals.ts, src/core/engine.ts, prompts/dispatch.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This witness concerns scoped input ownership. It does not establish general file-conflict detection or contention-aware scheduling across arbitrary worktrees.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate current deployment load and active commitments through operator decisions about concurrency, pause/resume and intervention on open tickets. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Queue pressure, stalled tickets, execution capacity and delivery problems across the deployment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change the operating capacity or suspend/resume the deployment and intervene in live commitments from its current view. - Decision owner: Deployment operator. The main session can request a missing ticket report and routes individual work, while the executor enforces configured capacity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Overview health and ticket projections, revisioned settings, worker-slot accounting, ticket stop/retry commands and the processing pass. - Closure path: The operator reads active load and commitments, saves an executor setting or pauses/resumes processing, or stops/retries a ticket. The runtime uses the changed setting for subsequent work, or terminates/replaces the assignment. - Boundary reachability: The shipped panel reads overview and calls management routes on the same runtime and store that govern dispatch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The main role&#39;s local accountability action asks an idle worker for a report. Retry and control settings require explicit human direction. The supported autonomous role has no evidenced independent deployment-wide resource or intervention decision loop. - Whole-system current view: The deployment overview combines open tickets, work awaiting a person, executor load, configured capacity and active operational problems. - Current-control decision scope: Global dispatch capacity and processing state, with operator intervention in individual commitments from that whole view. - Evidence: src/app/views/overview.ts, src/app/http.ts, src/app/settings.ts, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/app/tools/main.ts, docs/003-execution.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Pause governs new evaluation, dispatch and delivery. It is distinct from stopping already-running work.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Complementary examination of operational claims through a separately scoped evidence and judgment path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A worker&#39;s ordinary report may omit or mischaracterize the implementation result. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No bundled independent actor was found that examines the actual work and returns an audit judgment to deployment control. - Decision owner: No first-party audit owner established in this deployment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ticket and decision histories, model admission records, external PR review and check ingestion, and ordinary worker reports. - Closure path: External review/check feedback returns to the ticket worker. The main role consumes summaries and can request an ordinary missing report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The admission judgment concerns prospective requests. PR reviewers are external actors. The worker&#39;s own report and its assessment of review feedback are operational reporting. - Evidence: prompts/dispatch.md, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/sources/github-trigger.ts, src/app/tools/executor.ts, docs/003-execution.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: External CI or reviewer evidence can affect implementation. The deployment supplies no independent audit owner or a separately configured first-party audit role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Develop and return an adaptation option for the future capability of the deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in the deployment&#39;s environment that may call for a new capability or operating arrangement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No bundled loop was found that develops capability adaptation options from an external/prospective distinction and returns a decision to deployment control. - Decision owner: No first-party adaptation owner established at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source observation and replay, observe mode, model and source settings, and versioned operator prompt configuration. - Closure path: Observations become ticket decisions, including prospective accepts in observe mode. An operator can enable auto or edit settings and prompts using the management surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The main role assesses whether a concrete request fits current rules. The observe-to-auto recommendation is an operator deployment calibration step, with prospective ticket records rather than generated alternatives for changing deployment capability. - Evidence: docs/002-authorization.md, src/policy.ts, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/app/prompts.ts, SETUP.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The operator can use external analysis to change configuration. The boundary is the deployment&#39;s shipped operational loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Establish and revise the standing mandate for which repository demand the deployment may take up. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting or changing expectations about who may authorize work, which classes of changes are in scope, and whether inferred work may be undertaken. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the deployment-wide admission policy, allowed authorities, rule scope and mode. - Decision owner: The deployment operator, acting directly through the panel or by explicit configuration instruction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy and mode schema, settings revision, computed policy version, admission checks, affected-ticket notifications and later mandate revalidation. - Closure path: The operator changes the standing mandate after examining decision records or deployment needs. The saved version changes subsequent evaluation, queues stale evaluations anew, and sends relevant changes to affected active tickets. The executor revalidates ended work before resuming under its mandate. - Boundary reachability: The standard deployment exposes policy settings and decision records. Both the panel and explicit-request tool save through the runtime settings service read by evaluation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The main role applies the standing policy. Its settings tool is scoped to explicit human configuration requests, and assessed source content does not authorize a change in policy. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: The deployment&#39;s enduring authorization envelope for proactive repository work, beyond any individual ticket approval. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The deployment operator sets the policy, including in auto mode. - Return-to-operation path: Versioned settings feed the next admission decisions and revalidation; relevant changes are delivered to affected live workers. - Evidence: docs/002-authorization.md, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/app/settings.ts, src/core/engine.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A policy change does not immediately revoke all active mandates. Its effects depend on scope, notifications and the next decision or revalidation path.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/07848cd39bc6ed0eb563c11c2910fd627ec27ca6/assessments/ds/dsh-superagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 07848cd39bc6ed0eb563c11c2910fd627ec27ca6\">07848cd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:57:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mihazs/agent-harness-lab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness Lab</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mihazs/agent-harness-lab/tree/1b165594818bab9f92605fb4bd5ec0c6f943c2fc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b165594818bab9f92605fb4bd5ec0c6f943c2fc\">1b16559 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate bounded harness defects and generate an executable candidate instruction/skill change against project evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown agent failure mechanisms, contradictory observations, ineffective instructions, regressions and source-context gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the proposer model chooses which supported mechanism to address and what candidate file edits to stage; baseline/candidate model task executors choose actions under identical frozen tasks and runtime settings. - Decision owner: selected Codex/Claude/Responses model-backed proposer and task roles in explicitly invoked improve mode; first-party controller scopes and executes them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-local snapshots and path allowlists, model role prompts, source/analysis receipts, artifact hashes, shared trial/time ceiling, frozen candidate, paired executors and evaluation feedback. - Closure path: collect bounded evidence → proposer model selects candidate → change is frozen → baseline and candidate agent trials execute → observed failures/regressions feed the bounded refinement or final no-change/recommendation. - Boundary reachability: workflow.py invokes native/Responses role runner in preparation and task phases; ahl improve is the documented CLI entry, provided runtime availability and permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: proposer decides semantic repair hypotheses and actual candidate contents, not just a deterministic suggested text; analyze, static classifiers and test bookkeeping alone would be constructors, not S1 A. - Evidence: proposer dispatch and trial flow, controlled role preparation, documented modes. - Basis: pinned first-party execution code and runtime contract; not a live paid Codex/Claude experiment here. - Confidence: high that model-backed roles are shipped, medium for real-world repair quality. - Caveats: no model calls in analyze or offline diagnostics; no general automatic promotion implied by successful paired tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately established inter-S1 reciprocal conflict-attenuation loop beyond task role isolation and experiment scheduling at the defined single-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a proposer could contaminate evaluator inputs or a concurrent improvement run could alter the same project, but AHL prevents those situations by frozen views/locks rather than demonstrated feedback among independently operating S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: controller grants separate role paths, budgets, frozen snapshots and project locks; a stale or leaked input blocks the whole evaluation. - Decision owner: deterministic custody/permission controller, without cross-worker conflict-specific negotiation or corrective return to both S1 cells. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-scoped views, frozen task banks and hashes, project lock, candidate/baseline isolation, source-drift checks, sequential trial orchestration. - Closure path: potential cross-role contamination is prevented or detected; invalid runs stop or require intervention. This is evaluation integrity, not evidenced coordination of independent operational commitments. - Boundary reachability: role views, frozen candidate and validation are shipped; a separate correction negotiation between proposer and evaluator is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct fresh roles, sequential stages and file isolation are important safeguards, but not enough to infer VSM S2 without a concrete disturbance-feedback relation returning to affected S1 units. - Evidence: role construction and separation, controlled execution boundaries, preflight/custody policy. - Basis: bounded negative review of multi-role experiment/custody paths. - Confidence: medium-high for this recursion. - Caveats: a future multi-project adaptation fleet could implement true S2; that is outside this single-project run.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the active run&#39;s preparation/evaluation roles, resource budget, candidate eligibility, current evidence and terminal disposition, with user-owned runtime/scope initiation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budget exhaustion, changed source files, invalid evaluator controls, failing task bank, unavailable authentication, partial runs and repeated monitoring without new evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: controller freezes/validates candidate and grader inputs, schedules tests/refinement, rejects a candidate or emits recommendations; human owner authorizes project, chosen runtime, scope and any later behavioral adoption. - Decision owner: deterministic workflow.py and promotion prerequisites (C), with human operator (P) initiating and deciding outside-CLI adoption. Controller does not quietly grant itself publication rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project locks, shared 45-minute/24-role defaults, checksums, state receipts, role budgets, graded trial coverage, early fail-closed exits, optional monitor checkpoints. - Closure path: current evidence and role result → controller authorizes next preparation/test/stop or reports unsupported → stored checkpoint/receipt constrains the next agent dispatch. - Boundary reachability: ahl improve and ahl watch use these live controller functions; watch is foreground and must be explicitly started. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-run scheduling/disposition is rule-based despite model roles doing task work; real human controls authenticated invocation and reviewed candidate adoption. - Whole-system current view: baseline/candidate hashes, source identity, proposer/evaluator states, task bank coverage, model calls, time/trial remaining, results and previous monitor checkpoints. - Current-control decision scope: allow/deny a role run, protect frozen inputs, retry once, stop on invalid evidence, write final review/recommendation and watch restart suppression. - Evidence: controller loop, invocation preflight, monitoring mode. - Basis: first-party deterministic role/state controller. - Confidence: high in structural control rights; actual credentialed runs not repeated here. - Caveats: human adoption beyond CLI and real trusted production integration are not claimed as observed automatic completion.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separately prompted evaluator-author agent constructs tests/graders from baseline/source evidence while deliberately blind to the candidate, and the controller executes those frozen evaluators to challenge the proposer&#39;s claimed improvement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposer self-praise, overfit instruction rewrites, candidate-specific tests, evaluator leakage and differences that disappear on held-out/regression cases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluator-author model independently chooses test cases and grader construction within the baseline evidence and broken/partial/complete control contract; frozen graders then measure actual paired task outputs and can cause rejection or bounded refinement. - Decision owner: separate fresh model-backed evaluator-author role owns substantive test design; deterministic comparison controller enforces controls and verdict/disposition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate role workspace/views, baseline-only source context, withheld candidate and held-out inputs, grader self-tests, manifest freezing, task result artifacts, same-runtime paired trials and custody checks. - Closure path: proposer freezes candidate/claim → independently scoped author designs tests without seeing patch → controls/graders are frozen → baseline/candidate execute → actual result comparison rejects/permits refinement or emits review recommendation. - Boundary reachability: workflow.py constructs a separate evaluator session in native mode or construction.author/controlled role access, and validates a manifest before paired trials. No optional uninstalled reviewer service is required beyond the chosen agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deciding what independent probes should measure involves a different model context, not simply running a copy of the proposer&#39;s test; the final grader/veto itself is deterministic, so this is not automatically a certified independent human scientific audit. - Claim being audited: a proposed harness instruction/skill edit improves required task outcomes without regression under comparable inputs. - Ordinary reporting path: proposer mechanisms, candidate diff, self-declared proposal.json and any development trial feedback. - Complementary access path: evaluator author receives baseline/source/analysis without candidate patch, builds unrelated executable tasks and graders, and the controller examines fresh baseline/candidate task artifacts. - Independence boundary: roles are separately scoped and patch-blind by construction, but cooperative Codex/Claude runtime/hook behavior and model provider isolation are not proven adversarial security; some source metadata is shared. - Who acts on findings: controller rejects/refines/recommends; human decides any behavioral adoption. No claim of certified unconditional promotion. - Evidence: independent evaluation-construction prompt and calls, role-specific controlled author, frozen task method. - Basis: explicit shipped two-role model construction plus executable artifact feedback, not observed scientific effect size. - Confidence: medium-high for independent input view and actual verdict path, medium for model quality and native role isolation. - Caveats: “fresh session” alone is not a proof of full root independence or test correctness; graders need explicit self-tests and absence of leakage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no completed behavioral adoption return turning evaluated future-harness candidates into active project operating capability under the shipped CLI&#39;s general workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving task types and systematic future agent behavior might warrant new harness instructions and skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: proposer can forecast candidate effects and evaluation compares development/regression/held-out tasks, but improve deliberately returns a review recommendation instead of applying behavioral changes; the human may later edit active files separately. - Decision owner: user/project outside the CLI owns actual behavioral promotion; no first-party general S4 decision-to-adoption loop has authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retrospective analysis, selected candidate proposals, prospective task banks, held-out controls, promotion prerequisites and fail-closed recommendation status. - Closure path: candidate → prospective paired task evidence → review recommendation; missing final behavioral activation in the same supported controller means the full outside-and-then capability return is unclosed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: creating and evaluating future options is substantial S4-adjacent intelligence but cannot be scored as completed S4 when the proposed capability is explicitly not adopted. Narrow structural BOM repair is not general behavioral S4. - Evidence: README no auto-adoption, promotion policy recommendation-only, one-call narrow repair. - Basis: explicit runtime/README negative authority and supported-mode examination. - Confidence: high for CLI&#39;s deliberate blocked behavioral return; a manual fallback may differ under explicit user authority. - Caveats: installed harness-self-improvement skill describes assisted evaluation/editing; without a validated matching runtime/receipt this is not imported as general autonomous promotion into the guarded ahl improve path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate whole-project identity/ultimate-purpose adjudication loop evidenced in one harness optimization run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: privacy of traces, scope of external models, credentialed cost, whether an evaluator can be trusted and changes to global instructions are real governance concerns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: invocation permissions and configured scope restrict project files and runtime capabilities, while the user decides model/provider, accepts adoption and can stop the command; none is charter-level identity resolution returned as binding system purpose. - Decision owner: outside user/project authority; controller enforces safety/eligibility preconditions rather than deliberating ultimate purpose. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project path allowlists, no global skills by default, no publication rights, credential-bound external calls, privacy-safe artifacts and explicit fail-closed prerequisites. - Closure path: invalid permission/role scope blocks the run or preserves unmodified files; no first-party identity dispute reaches a higher legitimate policy owner and returns as an ultimate revised charter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety and provenance constraints limit experiments but are not themselves S5 governance. - Evidence: scope and limits, preflight boundaries, promotion authority. - Basis: bounded negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the human project owner has external policy rights; those are not automatically first-party runtime S5(P).\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/19d30e2238e1d50e87e240bcf67ab7b02ca7f8f6/assessments/ag/agent-harness-lab.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 19d30e2238e1d50e87e240bcf67ab7b02ca7f8f6\">19d30e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:08:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bread Companion</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion/tree/058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\">058ad15 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous bounded conversation and evidence-task transformation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different user material, missing/conflicting evidence, rejected interpretations and malformed or inappropriate generated candidates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose supported findings, wording, draft structure or a practice question; revise after concrete rejection/validation feedback. - Decision owner: the configured real-agent model within the first-party generation/repair loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompts, selected source segments, schema/evidence/style validators, two-attempt repair, run IDs and client lifecycle checks. - Closure path: validation errors re-enter a new generator attempt; accepted content becomes current findings/card/practice state, influencing later turns. - Boundary reachability: real-agent.generate implements the provider interface used by the deployed server; BreadController submits a current turn and reducer applies only matching epoch/context results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model determines substantive task content and correction, while the controller enforces validity and human save authority. Fixed UI transitions alone could not produce the same discretionary outcomes. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; prompts/agent.ts; domain/evidence.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no general tool-executing autonomy; drafts require user confirmation for persistence. This is not a therapy efficacy or clinical safety claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct operational-unit coordination path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These are one conversational work cell&#39;s pipeline/transport stages and revision-consistency controls. No distinct purposeful operational S1 cells with an inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation relation were found. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/run-controller.ts; services/call-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current organizational control function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These regulate individual requests, versions, service capacity and task consent. A session view or stop button is not by itself whole-system resource/commitment regulation with a function-specific parent decision loop. - Evidence: services/bread-controller.ts; services/run-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts; server/limits.ts; server/admission.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: narrow complementary semantic audit of whether contested analysis reintroduces the user&#39;s denied interpretation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a candidate can pass schema/verbatim/lexical checks yet paraphrase or recommend the rejected interpretation again. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently compare actual candidate with user rejection record and judge semantic consistency. - Decision owner: the model in the fresh rejection-review invocation; runtime parses/enforces its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate review prompt/request, only candidate/rejection/current user data, bounded audit input, conservative verdict parsing and forced retry/fallback. - Closure path: inconsistent/invalid audit adds REJECTEDINTERPRETATIONREPEATED; generator receives explicit corrective feedback; after a second repeated rejection the product returns quote/unknown fallback rather than display the contested interpretation. - Boundary reachability: RealAgentProvider invokes the separate reviewer only for non-listen analysis with rejectedFindings after normal validation; reducer REJECT and context-builder persist/send that rejection record. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent review call supplies semantic judgment beyond the generator&#39;s candidate and deterministic lexical check; parsing is enforcement, not the owner. No independent provider identity is claimed. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; server/providers/rejection-review.ts; domain/reducer.ts; domain/context-builder.ts; domain/evidence.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: same configured model/provider may serve both roles; independence is fresh context and task separation for this narrow consistency claim, not empirical independence from shared model biases. Ordinary chat is unaudited. - Claim being audited: the candidate respects the user&#39;s previously rejected interpretation and its paraphrases. - Ordinary reporting path: generator candidate passes schema/evidence/style and exact-normalized rejection checks. - Complementary access path: fresh semantic comparison of the actual full candidate against persisted user rejection records. - Independence boundary: separate stateless request with dedicated review instructions and no generator hidden reasoning/history; source of correction is user record, not a generator self-report. - Who acts on findings: the provider&#39;s repair loop withholds the candidate, requests revision and eventually substitutes a traceable non-interpretive fallback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective capability-adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These adjust current task content, context and service availability. They do not develop future environment/capability options and return an adaptation decision that changes the harness&#39;s present repertoire. Research/benchmark plans are adjacent. - Evidence: domain/context-builder.ts; domain/preferences.ts; server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity or ultimate-policy decision/return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static identity/permissions and user authority over task/content/save do not supply ultimate-policy arbitration. No identity-level issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate decision owner and returned as a new governing policy. - Evidence: prompts/agent.ts; domain/policy.ts; domain/reducer.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15/assessments/br/bread-companion.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15\">8c0178f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:54:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jirathip-dev/herdsman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">herdsman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jirathip-dev/herdsman/tree/efd970e817c2021439c5415739e695f4af119b1d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision efd970e817c2021439c5415739e695f4af119b1d\">efd970e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: configured coding agent lanes implement and repair requested changes; separately tasked review agents exercise real task-specific judgment on code/tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new issue requirements, unknown codebase state, implementation errors, test failures and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which code and tests to change, what fixes to make and how to respond with evidence to reviewer findings within the owner-authorized issue. - Decision owner: model-based implementer and, for their bounded verification output, reviewer roles launched under the fleet pipeline; the deterministic drive merely chooses when to launch the next stage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: herdr worktree/pane spawn, issue brief, role prompts, profile hooks, PR cache, head-pinned verdicts and event log. - Closure path: issue queued → implementer writes/tests/pushes change → reviewer evaluates exact head → FAIL returns findings to fix lane or PASS and green CI lead through merge gate → code reaches integration branch. - Boundary reachability: drive and timers launch the roles by supported fleet settings, and the named default pipeline wires their outputs back to future steps; this is not a unit-test-only role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code edits, targeted tests and adversarial judgments require meaningful agent choices; herdsman&#39;s hard transition and merge checks are constructor enforcement, not additional autonomous S1 judgment. - Evidence: role instructions, spawn source, pipeline. - Basis: documented supported operation plus pinned role and spawn wiring. - Confidence: high for model role construction, bounded for outcomes without a provider-backed run in this review. - Caveats: no agent executes without an installed, authenticated external harness and herdr; no unattended intelligence is attributed to the Rust scheduler itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: first-party conflict preview, serial integration gate and isolated lane worktrees attenuate specific interference among concurrently coding issue units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two issue agents editing overlapping files or branches cause rejected merges, overwrite/ordering hazards and repeated updates against the integration branch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect file-level collisions with git merge-tree, show the specific conflicting paths, order ready PRs, enforce required serial groups and return the losing lane to update its branch before another reviewed merge. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic overlap, drive, gate and serial-group rules; no model has a standing right to settle portfolio-wide branch conflicts by changing these policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated worktrees, cached head-pair collision preview, HEADS-UP prompts, exact-head verdicts, serial gate and update rounds with bounded retry. - Closure path: conflicting concurrent heads → warning/serial ordering and first merge → implementer of later head receives update instruction and incorporates integration → fresh reviewer verdict and CI → next eligible merge. - Boundary reachability: the installed drive calls overlap preview before action ordering, and the supported conflict/behind path actively spawns an update role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concrete cross-issue contention is treated, but collision detection and merge precedence are coded invariants, not an autonomous S2 decision owner; this is C. - Distinct S1 units: independently running implementer lanes for different issue worktrees in the same configured fleet. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file edits and stale branch ancestry can make otherwise valid S1 output conflict at integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: file-level preview and first-ready ordering, strict serial marking, integration update and mandatory fresh review. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: later implementer gets exact conflicting paths/updated integration instructions, edits its work and pushes a changed head for another review. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation recognizes actual write-set/merge interference between independent S1 agents and closes that conflict through a change in what the later agent does. - Evidence: overlap source, drive conflicts and ordering, lane conflict/update contract. - Basis: directly implemented collision-specific runtime path and prompt feedback. - Confidence: high for structural conflict attenuation; no claim of perfect prediction or autonomous negotiation. - Caveats: an early heads-up is advisory and does not lock all overlapping files; a first reviewed merge may still force an update round.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the human fleet owner sees the live issue/lane/host portfolio and can pause or resume a fleet, intervene in blocked work and decide current resource and merge envelopes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: excessive concurrent lanes, host pressure, stalled fixes, owner questions and unwanted work on active issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: use the phone board/CLI to pause or resume, move/park an issue, answer owner asks, extend review rounds or grant the narrow owner override without bypassing exact-head review and CI. - Decision owner: fleet owner/operator. drive computes the next mechanically valid transition, but does not set purpose/priorities as an AI metasystem. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live status, board and need-you cards, logged pause/decision events, queue moves, host admission and owner-only intervention controls. - Closure path: operator inspects current state → changes fleet pause/queue/decision or permitted round limit → next drive/kick reads event state and changes or withholds operational lane work. - Boundary reachability: pause, resume, ask, answer and board actions are shipped CLI/server paths and drive reads the same event log, not aspirational UI only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human selects priorities and discretionary intervention; programmed drive and resource thresholds do not upgrade to S3 A. - Whole-system current view: board shows queue, pipeline, lanes, host load, waiting and needs-you across a fleet. - Current-control decision scope: current issue order, which work waits or proceeds, review rounds, host/cost envelope and capacity within configured policy. - Evidence: owner board and drive behavior, live host control, pause/answer implementation. - Basis: explicit human command path and recorded return to scheduler. - Confidence: high for supported owner mode; model-directed supervisor not established. - Caveats: UI requires a running server/paired device; same control also exists through CLI.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent, adversarial reviewer agents inspect implementation code and objective acceptance evidence outside the implementer&#39;s self-report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but incorrect implementation claims, tests proving only mocks, leaked secrets and unreviewed scope changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: read the actual diff and criteria, independently rerun tests and return a head-specific PASS/FAIL with blocking findings that the implementer must address. - Decision owner: a separately launched read-only reviewer model in a distinct lane and role/profile; verdict and CI checks merely enforce validity and preserve its findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated reviewer worktree, read-only pre-tool hook, verdict.md with reviewed 40-hex head and tests checked, exact-head gate and fresh check-round spawn. - Closure path: implementation PR head → reviewer inspects raw code/tests/criteria independently → head-pinned verdict and findings → implementer fix or approved integration → new head invalidates prior verdict and is independently reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: default and custom pipelines include independent check stages, distinct from build, with verified role constraints and drive spawns on pushed heads. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adversarial technical judgment belongs to a distinct model role that can reject claims, not a boolean test wrapper or the implementer&#39;s own confirmation. - Claim being audited: implementation actually meets issue acceptance criteria and tests/PR description prove it. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer diff, test report and PR claim. - Complementary access path: independent checkout/diff, direct tests and head evidence against the original issue brief. - Independence boundary: reviewer role is prohibited from writing code, commits, pushes and PR comments, uses its own lane and verifies the actual current head; the same external harness vendor may still provide both models. - Who acts on findings: the subsequent implementer fix lane; herdsman&#39;s event/gate machine blocks merge until fresh PASS and green CI. - Evidence: reviewer role, review stage isolation, verdict source. - Basis: mandatory shipped review workflow and access separation. - Confidence: high for implemented audit path; reviewer accuracy not measured here. - Caveats: separate role/worktree provides process and tool isolation, not external institutional independence; no live paid provider review was performed in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective outside-environment interpretation and return into enduring fleet capability changes was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new coding models, repository ecosystems, infrastructure economics and future workloads might eventually require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no internal future-environment option selection and promotion right evidenced; host choices and code paths are bounded to present work/load. - Decision owner: not established for a separate S4 loop; fleet configuration and infrastructure upgrade choices remain with humans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: host-history sampling, cost ceiling, doctor, backlog, context prompts and issue queue. - Closure path: observed present host pressure can deny a lane or start a configured cloud overflow host, but no projected external future becomes a selected enduring fleet strategy returned to implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: capacity observation is near-term S3/operational admission, and repository improvement by contributors is outside the installed fleet recursion. - Evidence: host placement and budget, architecture and scope. - Basis: bounded negative source review of shipped operating modes. - Confidence: medium-high for missing S4 closure in the reviewed modes. - Caveats: future model/host choice by the operator is possible but not credited as installed autonomous S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party loop was established that brings an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue to legitimate authority and returns the binding result to govern the fleet as a coherent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing the fleet&#39;s ultimate mission, reconciling external future adaptation with current obligations, or revising the constitutional distribution of authority could require such a loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the owner may edit fleet.toml and decide scoped owner asks, but no shipped identity-level agenda or decision-return path was evidenced beyond those task and administration actions. - Decision owner: not established for S5 at the declared fleet recursion; ultimate responsibility remains with the human owner outside the demonstrated herdsman function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner/routine ask classifier, OWNERTOPIC refusal, no-default owner answer, protected branches, command hooks, limit settings and fleet configuration. - Closure path: owner decisions are returned to individual tasks and settings bind future runs, but there is no demonstrated loop from an organization-level identity dispute through deliberation to a revised ultimate charter applied by the fleet. - Boundary reachability: task-level owner asks and configuration commands are shipped; their existence is not by itself S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval for secrets, cost or scope during a single implementation may be important safety governance without constituting identity/policy-level VSM S5. - Evidence: owner/routine decision contract, ask/answer code, fleet policy and gate. - Basis: bounded negative first-party review, distinguishing task authorization from ultimate-policy closure. - Confidence: medium-high for this scoped absence; an independently organized human governance layer is not ruled out. - Caveats: this does not deny that an operator can govern or change herdsman outside the built fleet workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2500cd237846b78303b459e41ab53d20a64b0f13/assessments/he/herdsman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2500cd237846b78303b459e41ab53d20a64b0f13\">2500cd2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:39:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Beyon-Digital/control-plane\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Beyon Control Plane</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Beyon-Digital/control-plane/tree/9dbe4012bdf8b2d2ee333402912b408aadd35e6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9dbe4012bdf8b2d2ee333402912b408aadd35e6d\">9dbe401 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: provider coding agents work on repo tasks through native worker session and tool/result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, compilation failures, tool restrictions, interrupted provider processes and changing workspace state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each configured provider agent chooses coding actions and follow-up responses in its provider session, limited by its own approval policy and worker exposure; the first-party worker accepts, persists and reports concrete execution events. - Decision owner: model-backed Codex/Claude/Kiro agent; external provider inference is a runtime dependency, while Beyon owns the adapter, execution and feedback boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider stdio/sidecar adapters, scoped session creation, event stream, workspace tools, idempotency receipts, device authorization and abort. - Closure path: prompt is accepted by worker → provider agent works with permitted tools → worker persists events and file effects → operator/orchestrator reads outcome and can issue follow-up or cancellation. - Boundary reachability: README documents installed providers and worker start; orchestrator&#39;s concrete createsession and promptsession routes call authenticated v1/sessions endpoints. The provider must separately be installed/authenticated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the provider agent selects how to do coding work rather than simply replaying deterministic scripts; host safety and networking alone are not the autonomous decision. - Evidence: run and provider boundary, provider adapters, orchestrator session calls. - Basis: source and supported deployment documentation; no live provider inference run. - Confidence: high for reachability; real account/provider integration is not independently proven. - Caveats: the external agents&#39; internal organizational functions are not copied into first-party Beyon ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced inter-S1 conflict-specific reciprocal attenuation loop between independently acting coding agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent sessions could edit shared files or compete for worker resources, but the reviewed implementation does not show a first-party overlap analyzer and corrective return to the competing operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the orchestrator&#39;s tool calls are sequential and worker policies bound sessions/concurrency; neither explicitly identifies contested S1 obligations and reconciles them with collision feedback. - Decision owner: no distinct inter-S1 coordination owner established; session limits are deterministic worker enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential model calls, per-account isolation, role grants, session ownership, worker quotas, Git conditional saves and separate worktrees. - Closure path: requests are authenticated and serialized locally where required; a resource denial returns an error, not an evidenced negotiation/adjustment between multiple S1 agents. - Boundary reachability: concurrency and host/account controls are shipped but do not themselves prove inter-S1 regulatory coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: having many sessions, a central orchestrator and an action cap is insufficient without identified actual collisions and a feedback relation specifically resolving those collisions. - Evidence: orchestrator tool loop, worker controls, workspace operations. - Basis: bounded first-party negative inspection. - Confidence: medium; a model might manually notice a conflict, but a reproducible supported collision workflow is not demonstrated. - Caveats: explicit separate worktrees reduce collision probability; they do not automatically constitute S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: a client-side main orchestrator can inspect the present portfolio of installed agents, active sessions, progress and host account capabilities, and direct current coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tasks accepted but not completed, unavailable providers, failed/uncertain sessions, unexpected file effects, new user priorities and permission changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: when management is enabled, the main model can select a provider, create sessions, prompt them and interrupt a live run after querying hostinfo, listsessions, sessionevents, workspacediff and other evidence. Human retains scope and approval rights. - Decision owner: separate model-backed first-party OrchestratorEngine for operational session-portfolio decisions; human owner supplies binding agent-management permission, host/account selection and provider/tool approvals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped API client, model tool catalog/dispatcher allowlist, per-turn 10-round/40-call budget, user toggle, persistent worker session events and host access checks. - Closure path: current host/session/workspace evidence → orchestrator chooses create/prompt/interrupt or wait/inspect → worker accepts/rejects → event states return to subsequent model judgment and user UI. - Boundary reachability: OrchestratorEngine.run calls configured model inference and OrchestratorTools.execute implements actual endpoints, available from the native Orchestrator screen; read-only mode removes management calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current portfolio management is an explicit model decision layer distinct from a worker&#39;s deterministic dispatch; notation includes (P) because the user holds the management enablement and unapproved tool authority. - Whole-system current view: worker host/provider discovery, per-session states/events, files and Git state across the connected account. - Current-control decision scope: creation, follow-up prompting, interruption and current inspection; not identity policy, user consent overrides or provider permission approval. - Evidence: executable tool catalog and dispatch, orchestrator limits and scope. - Basis: first-party model loop and scoped live API wiring; not a successful real model-provider performance claim. - Confidence: medium-high for implemented mode, with uncertainty about account entitlement and real deployment operation. - Caveats: without a connected worker or with management disabled, this S3 agent&#39;s action right is absent; these modes are not silently credited with full orchestration.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide evidence independent of coding-agent prose about actual session acceptance/completion, worker state and Git working-tree differences, enabling a corrective decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent can claim a task is done when a prompt was only accepted or its changes are missing; client state can be stale after disconnect/restart. - Decisive decision or feedback right: orchestrator/human can query durable worker sessionevents, listsessions and raw workspacediff, compare to the coding agent&#39;s story and then change follow-up or interrupt; the alternative evidence path itself is deterministic rather than a dedicated autonomous auditor. - Decision owner: independent first-party worker event/actual-file evidence constructor (C); model orchestrator or human interprets it, but the code does not guarantee a distinct adversarial auditing-model verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable SQLite events and receipts, restart uncertain rather than success/replay, actual workspace Git diff endpoint, clear prompt acceptance semantics. - Closure path: provider report/accepted prompt → fresh worker events and raw Git diff challenge the claimed state → main orchestrator/user can send a correction or halt a session → next provider action and events reflect the adjustment. - Boundary reachability: model tool catalog includes these live read-only tools, backed by worker API and stored events; the docs explicitly instruct inspection rather than treating acceptance as completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these observations do not originate in the coding agent&#39;s natural-language assertion, yet no separate reviewer model automatically produces a verdict; hence a complementary constructor, not S3 A. - Claim being audited: the provider agent actually completed requested work and current workspace effects match its claimed progress. - Ordinary reporting path: provider messages, prompt-accepted notifications and local client conversation state. - Complementary access path: persisted host event cursor/status and independent Git filesystem diff read through the authenticated worker. - Independence boundary: coding agents can change workspace files and might manipulate their own outputs; the event store is separately maintained by the worker, but does not prove correctness, and actual Git diff alone cannot validate tests. - Who acts on findings: parent human or client-side orchestrator issues a new prompt, pauses, or explicitly declines completion claims. - Evidence: orchestrator independent read tools, durable events and restart semantics, worker store. - Basis: first-party separate observation and optional corrective return, not an observed independent semantic audit. - Confidence: medium: robust source separation, discretionary rather than mandatory corrective use. - Caveats: an orchestrator can choose not to inspect, and a diff can miss semantics; no guaranteed code-review safety threshold is inferred.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no packaged externally prospective scenario/option selection feeding durable changes to future operating capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future agent/provider availability, model eligibility, host topology and longer-term developer workflows may shift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans select current model profiles/endpoints, connect hosts and install workers; model discovery and account authorization are present-day capability choices, not a strategic outside-and-then return loop. - Decision owner: developer/operator configuration; no distinct first-party S4 intelligence decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model profiles and discovery, platform release/updater, host management, installation and verification guides. - Closure path: a user can switch a model or host and run the current orchestrator, but there is no shown future environment options assessment that binds a changed long-term operating capability through S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: discovering current models and installing signed updates are not VSM S4 merely because they concern technological change. - Evidence: model setup, architecture and update trust. - Basis: bounded negative review of shipped model/settings, current orchestrator and future release modes. - Confidence: medium-high at declared host/account recursion. - Caveats: developer roadmap and update mechanisms can evolve without proving a deployed prospective adaptation regulator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced ultimate identity/purpose policy conflict adjudicated by a legitimate organizational authority and returned to runtime commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting device trust, human permission and long-term assistant identity may require parent policy, but runtime currently applies already configured controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: administrators enroll/revoke devices and grant roles; the user enables agent management, accepts provider permissions and can emergency-stop. These are scoped operational/security rights, not identity-policy adjudication. - Decision owner: outside human user/account administrator and project governance; no shipped distinct S5 decision loop with a charter-level return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TLS device identity, role/session ACL, certificate renewal, worker privilege boundaries, explicit trusted mode and emergency stop. - Closure path: permission changes apply to future API calls and revoke streams, without a witnessed foundational identity dispute being raised, decided and returned as ultimate policy across the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: revocation and human override are genuine safeguards but not sufficient S5 closure; no autonomous S5 is inferred from a secure product name or operator veto. - Evidence: trust model and deployment limits, human agent-management bounds. - Basis: bounded negative inspection of actual governance/permissions rather than contributor organization. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: multi-user administrators can govern security policies, but that is not an evidenced question-to-legitimate-S5-to-binding-operational-return loop at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8ba36072b4c05023dce5352227d9a0bad1958d31/assessments/be/beyon-control-plane.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8ba36072b4c05023dce5352227d9a0bad1958d31\">8ba3607 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T21:22:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mdiri-ai/SoduWork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SoduWork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mdiri-ai/SoduWork/tree/97b9c2118a966869a862ef3aa3181ccb77c3dd3b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 97b9c2118a966869a862ef3aa3181ccb77c3dd3b\">97b9c21 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement assigned code or document tasks and inspect resulting local artifacts using model-selected actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel requirements, repository contents, command results, implementation errors, changed code and review feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured agent model selects its next coding/research actions and subsequent corrections within the dispatched task; SoduWork&#39;s optional Harness² text adapter also uses models to propose task-method changes before actual work. - Decision owner: configured external agent runtime for the substantive execution, not Hub&#39;s deterministic scheduler; SoduWork owns the packaged dispatch/receipt/rework integration, but does not claim the third-party agent&#39;s inner loop as original code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: aiexec.dispatchsync, Runner adapter, task-specific isolated worktree, persisted workflow/outbox, fixed artifact receipts, task context, retry caps and execution policy. - Closure path: Hub task/approved plugin AI node → configured Runner invokes model-driven agent → agent observes repository/tools and performs work → versioned commit or file receipt returns to Hub → review feedback can dispatch a further agent work attempt. - Boundary reachability: README&#39;s supported Runner/agent integrations and bundled starter implement node connect this operating path to the ordinary self-hosted distribution; it is not merely an unshipped extension point. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model judgment controls execution contents and rework; the Hub&#39;s graph, permissions and delivery checks constrain and record these decisions but do not author them. S1 ownership is at the composite supported deployment boundary, not a claim of first-party model-loop implementation. - Evidence: starter plan and implementation, AI dispatch and pinned workspace, external execution adapters, text-only probe. - Basis: explicit composition and code reachability. - Confidence: high in dispatch and closure wiring; medium in generality across installed external agent kinds. - Caveats: no live paid-provider or full deployment test; a Hub installation with zero configured agents has no executing S1, and the external runtime implements inner decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: separate concurrently dispatched agent task executions, each owning its local model-directed implementation work on a registered Runner. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two or more independent task executions compete for bounded Runner capacity; a new dispatch can receive RUNNERATCAPACITY while another S1 holds execution slots. - Attenuating coordination relation: Hub&#39;s Runner-capability and tag routing, explicit capacity-busy classification and persisted outbox retry defer the displaced task instead of falsely marking it executed or corrupting the incumbent. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after backoff and renewed admission the displaced S1 is actually started, processes the original task context and returns a real receipt; failure/busy is visible to the orchestration state meanwhile. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the source handles a named, observable interference among distinct operational task units over a finite common execution resource, and uses the returned capacity signal to alter when the next unit may act. - Function: attenuate runner-execution interference between separate agent tasks competing for bounded Runner capacity, allowing later S1 work to proceed without pretending a rejected dispatch succeeded. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous AI nodes requiring the same finite Runner slots, RUNNERATCAPACITY, unavailable/incompatible machines and delayed remote acknowledgements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party constructor-defined capacity routing, busy classification and scheduled requeue; there is no demonstrated model-owned arbitration of shared capacity. - Decision owner: SoduWork&#39;s deterministic Hub scheduler and agent-route selection, as constructed by the developer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runner pick/capability scope and capacity result, RunnerCapacityBusy, requeuecapacitywait, persisted outbox claims and retry timing. - Closure path: multiple active AI node dispatches → a Runner reports capacity busy → Hub classifies rather than fails task work → moves waiting dispatch back to eligible queue → later capacity permits agent operation and receipt collection. - Boundary reachability: production workflowengine.executeai handles RUNNERATCAPACITY; the normal outbox tick path invokes capacity retry handling for registered Runner deployments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this specific cross-task resource conflict and recovery path is packaged and operational, but its conflict handling is hard-coded rather than agent-decided, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: Runner busy classification and dispatch, Runner selection and execution, outbox recovery. - Basis: structural constructive, with a concrete capacity-collision witness. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is narrow execution-capacity coordination; authored DAG ordering or agent-to-reviewer handoff alone would not suffice for S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Whole-system current view: the Hub&#39;s multi-task issue/assignment/status board and live workflow state expose ongoing team commitments, failed work and available owner routes across issues rather than just one agent turn. - Current-control decision scope: a legitimate human team operator can reassign current work, change priority and decide which stopped/ready issue to resume within the workspace&#39;s common operational capacity; the updates return through task state and assignment notifications. - Function: maintain team-wide current issue commitments, ownership and priority, and return human portfolio intervention to the working operational cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple simultaneous team requests, unassigned work, failed or blocked workflows, changing priorities and responsibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a human team operator edits issue priority/assignment or chooses the next start/rework; Hub enforces authorization and pushes the changed assignment into the relevant task/agent dispatch path. - Decision owner: legitimate workspace operator/user in the shipped first-party parent-managed mode, not an autonomous S3 model. Automated scheduling and status transitions implement but do not own the portfolio judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multi-task issue views, tasks update API with priority and assigneeUserId, task arrival notifications, workflow failure state, board status and user approval gates. - Closure path: Hub issue list/current workflow state exposes portfolio → operator revises priority, ownership or authorization across team issues → tasks persists change and emits assignment events → later task dispatch/owner view follows revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: the Hub/board and task mutation APIs ship with the standard multi-user application; the operator can close this parent loop without editing source or installing an extra plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S3 is parent-owned human current control (P), while executor models remain bounded S1 agents. A human approval on one artifact alone is not the asserted S3 witness; the cross-issue state/commitment path is. - Evidence: task fields and reassignment, task and workflow state, team workspace UX. - Basis: parent-governed operating mode, structurally traced. - Confidence: medium; project/workspace permissions and actual operator behavior vary by deployment. - Caveats: no autonomous whole-current controller is established, and task-list visibility alone without reassignment/priority return would not qualify.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: an implementation agent&#39;s delivered commit is correct, secure, maintainable and satisfies the task&#39;s acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementer returns a git revision/receipt and its own status through the Runner to the workflow engine; that result is not treated as sufficient audit evidence. - Complementary access path: the independent aireview node is dispatched with the immutable commit and a separate isolated worktree, and instructed to read that repository reality directly and produce a fresh code-review.json rather than reuse an upstream completion summary. - Independence boundary: a distinct review-node attempt with its own workspace, code read rights, pinned target, read-only/no-merge instruction and fresh structured findings; runtime configuration may still use the same vendor/model, so the claim is operational independence of evidence and action, not guaranteed model diversity. - Who acts on findings: workflowengine validates the review report, records attestation on approval or fails the node and returns change requests to implementation rework, subject to bounded retry/acceptance gates. - Function: independently examine an implementation&#39;s fixed code artifact against task acceptance and code quality requirements, and issue corrective findings to current execution control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect, insecure or unmaintainable implementations and false completion claims from the original executing agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately dispatched reviewer agent reads the pinned code revision, forms an AI-authored structured verdict and findings; the Hub validates evidence shape and deterministically initiates rework on changesrequested. - Decision owner: independent review-node model for the substantive audit judgment; Hub owns immutable target enforcement, report validation, attestation and rework routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled aireview node separate from implement, per-node isolated worktree, fixed codeReviewTarget commit, no-write reviewer instruction, fresh code-review.json, workflowinputs.codereviewreport, and rejection/rework machinery. - Closure path: implementation commit receipt → reviewer dispatched to inspect fixed commit directly → code-review.json with verdict/findings → Hub refuses success on changes requested and returns findings to new implementation attempt → revised commit can be independently checked again. - Boundary reachability: the default starter plugin includes mandatory independent AI review before human acceptance; workflowengine supplies pinned-version review and processes its structured result in the normal live node polling path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model makes the fact-finding and correctness judgment with complementary access to the artifact itself, rather than trusting the implementation agent&#39;s status summary. Deterministic JSON checks and retry routing are enforcement, not the audit decision. - Evidence: shipped independent review stage, review isolation and locked target, review verdict/attestation processing. - Basis: explicit agent audit and corrective closure. - Confidence: medium-high for isolated audit path; medium for strong reviewer-model/provider independence in every installation. - Caveats: different node identity and isolated checkout do not guarantee different underlying foundation model; a review plugin that skips direct artifact examination would not satisfy this state in a particular run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no product-owned loop is established that prospectively scans environmental futures, selects adaptation options and returns changes to the team&#39;s future operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new plugin versions, agent providers, requested future work and changing task methods can arise, but these are administered or triggered per task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operators install or approve plugins, choose agents and methods; the optional Harness² adapter refines one immediate task plan, not future system adaptation policy. - Decision owner: human plugin/operator configuration and task-level model planning; no separate S4 authority is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin catalog, version review, Harness² planning probe, task briefs and model-agent routing. - Closure path: changed task prompts or manually installed capability can reach next execution; no environmental forecast → option portfolio → prospective capability decision → operating return is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-method improvement (including text-only Harness² probes) does not by itself change the team&#39;s future-facing viable capability. - Evidence: Harness² text refinement, plugin governance. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a custom future-intelligence plugin could instantiate S4 beyond the reviewed packaged boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no completed identity or ultimate-policy decision concerning the team as a viable whole is shown within the shipped operational boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe plugin capabilities, delivery permissions, worker access, approval rights and artifact release are constrained by policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans grant access, approve individual deliveries and authorize plugin versions; static rule checks reject prohibited operations but do not adjudicate an identity/ultimate-policy conflict. - Decision owner: users and configured enforcement for ordinary authorization; no evidenced S5 matter is escalated to a legitimate ultimate-policy holder and returned as governing identity policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin governance queues, release-control approvals, role/permission checks, audit trail and runner admission. - Closure path: approval allows or blocks a plugin/task/release; there is no demonstrated organization-identity conflict → S5 judgment → changed governing mandate propagated to S3/S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: source-backed approval gates and access controls are real but not sufficient proof of S5 decision ownership. - Evidence: plugin governance, approval-driven task acceptance, workflow approval gates. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an organization may use these controls to implement external S5 governance, but that parent arrangement is not established by this source alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/23512ba54ae8461def5469ae68f5313aff017461/assessments/so/soduwork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 23512ba54ae8461def5469ae68f5313aff017461\">23512ba &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:39:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/7ar-chat/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">7ar harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/7ar-chat/harness/tree/89edbffde74f4aa2c7675dc07615f26ab76cae91\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 89edbffde74f4aa2c7675dc07615f26ab76cae91\">89edbff &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: a model-directed run selects and uses tools to produce the requested task outcome in a sandbox/host session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: diverse coding/information requests, filesystem and tool observations, failures, model responses and approval boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool calls, interpret results, continue/replan, produce task response or a code change/PR. - Decision owner: the configured live LLM-backed agent in each session or factory task; delegated agents own their bounded subtask choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ai/agent.Loop, sandbox adapters, tool registry, steering, durable events, approvals and per-task policy/budget hooks. - Closure path: model action → sandbox/tool result and event → next model turn or task conclusion; factory end hook records state, may request follow-up PR work. - Boundary reachability: the CLI and embedded/web hosts compose the loop; FactoryFeature installs a spawner and task dispatcher in the supported FACTORY=1 mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the task work; queueing, policies, tool execution and approvals constrain but do not select the operational response. - Evidence: README quick start, single loop architecture, factory spawner and runtime hook. - Basis: structural; source paths and scripted tests, no live external model result claimed. - Confidence: high for first-party ownership/wiring. - Caveats: local backend does not itself isolate the host; a model key and applicable runtime configuration are required for real inference.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate conflicts among distinct task agents that share repository integration, dependencies and constrained execution capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing coding sessions/branches may conflict with the shared base, dependent tasks may start before prerequisites, and concurrent jobs may contend for quotas. - Decisive decision or feedback right: gate dependent task release, detect CI/merge conflicts, return actionable rebase/test feedback and defer incompatible integration; a whole-inter-S1 autonomous coordinator is not established. - Decision owner: deterministic factory task/forge mechanisms for admission and conflict detection; the receiving task agent decides its own fix, while the human/task policy controls merge permission. A function-specific constructor path exists, not a demonstrated autonomous S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conditional task-state transitions, DependsOn release, MaxConcurrent, per-PR Mergeable/CI checks and MaxFixRounds feedback loop. - Closure path: dependency completion releases the waiting S1; an integration conflict or failing CI leaves the PR unmerged and passes feedback to the producing agent, which can rebase/fix and publish a new head for re-evaluation. - Boundary reachability: FactoryFeature composes tasks.Service and forge.Loop; these are invoked by the supported factory mode, not generic interfaces alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a repo-level S1 interaction is specifically regulated, but the decisions to defer/settle are mechanical or owner-governed; assigning model discretion to a named coordinator would overread the code. - Distinct S1 units: separate factory task agent sessions pursuing changes against one project repository, including bounded delegated sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: changes originating in different cells can invalidate a common base or require an ordering relation, and unchecked parallel execution can exceed current shared limits. - Attenuating coordination relation: dependency blocking/release and conflict-aware PR integration with CI/result/merge-state feedback, rather than unguarded simultaneous publication. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ciFailed or conflictFeedback tells the same task agent to inspect, rebase/fix, test and reopen the PR; stored task state and checks are consulted before the next release or merge. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness concerns actual cross-cell interference at shared repository integration and prerequisite constraints; report collection, a tool queue or generic delegatemany alone is not being counted as S2. - Evidence: dependency state and release, CI and conflict feedback/resume, delegation isolation/limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; inter-S1 interference/closure is explicit, agent-owned system-wide coordination is not. - Caveats: the factory documents fake-host end-to-end coverage, not verified production-scale contention resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: whole-current oversight and interventions over an installed user&#39;s/project&#39;s agent sessions in supported team mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked approvals, stuck/failing sessions, priority of attention and conflicting active work commitments visible across sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which session to continue, steer, approve/deny, cancel or start, after an all-visible-session view. - Decision owner: the legitimate authenticated user/project member authorizes risky acts; the supervisor agent observes and recommends but does not independently execute these acts without user confirmation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listsessions, readsession, watcher, supervised session tools, tool approval gate, membership access checks, audit and event feed. - Closure path: user examines the supervisor&#39;s system-wide status/readout → authorizes its concrete action → sessionsend/approve/cancel/spawn changes an ongoing run, its permission hold or the current work inventory; audit records the decision. - Boundary reachability: harness-web -team installs the first-party app/product Supervisor; a watcher can proactively surface exceptions, and the user can converse with the supervisor in its dedicated thread. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based attention triage is present, but every material intervention tool is explicitly registered as risky and requires the human&#39;s confirmation in this mode; runtime budgets/dispatch alone are not S3 authority. - Whole-system current view: overviewFor and listSessions aggregate accessible personal/project sessions with status, project, creator, current wait and update time, augmented by readsession context. - Current-control decision scope: human-approved correction/termination, unblock and allocation of new session work across the user&#39;s visible installed operation; this is not a claim over unrelated tenant organizations. - Evidence: supervisor contract and risky approvals, whole-current list and acting tools, team settings. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the explicitly enabled human-owned mode. - Caveats: FACTORY=1 and -team are opt-in; a standalone one-session CLI has no asserted S3 loop. Factory queue/scheduler is not counted as autonomous S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the coding agent&#39;s completion claim before integration, using a direct code-host diff and a configured different reviewer model. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent may submit defective, insecure or task-incomplete changes while its own summary or CI gives an incomplete account. - Decisive decision or feedback right: emit approve/reject and findings on the exact PR; block merge or return fix feedback to the producing task. - Decision owner: a separately configured FACTORYREVIEWMODEL with explicit different-model enforcement for that task; mechanical security/test checks are supporting guards. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge direct PR diff/comments, mechanical protected-path/secrets/injection checks, CI-success requirement, model review rubric, head-specific recorded verdict and guarded merge. - Closure path: CI-ready PR → independent diff/reviewer judgment → forge.Loop.gate posts a verdict → approval allows guarded merge (only if AutoMerge) or findings resume/block the authoring S1, affecting the next attempt. - Boundary reachability: FactoryFeature installs the review gate when Forge is configured and review is not disabled; setting a distinct ReviewModel explicitly requires reviewer/author model inequality. This is a supported installed mode, not a borrowed external organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the configured reviewer model judges a code-host artifact without relying on the producing agent&#39;s report; default fallback to the same model is explicitly weaker and not credited as independent. - Claim being audited: the authoring factory agent&#39;s PR actually satisfies its task and is safe to integrate. - Ordinary reporting path: the author/agent task result and PR/CI status used by the normal factory work loop. - Complementary access path: the gate fetches actual PR patch/comments from GitHub, runs independent static checks and asks a distinct model to inspect the diff. - Independence boundary: distinct configured model checked by RequireDifferentModel, outside the producer&#39;s agent conversation; only this mode is counted, not the default same-model fallback. - Who acts on findings: factory PR loop withholds merging, publishes feedback and restarts the authoring task, or blocks for a human; successful verdict remains SHA-specific. - Evidence: reviewer distinct-model and direct-diff checks, gate feedback and guarded merge, factory feature review wiring, documented same-model caveat and setup limitation. - Basis: structural, conditional on the documented distinct-reviewer mode. - Confidence: medium; strongly wired source path, no actual independent live provider review was executed for this assessment. - Caveats: NoReview disables the gate; without a separate reviewer model the default self-review explicitly warns that it is not independent. Project CI and GitHub are complementary evidence sources, not ownership of the audit judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated installed decision loop that interprets external future change and selects/returns a prospective capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new future tasks, repo practices, provider conditions and failures can require changed capabilities; present modes primarily address active or past tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4-specific prospective adaptation judgment with a returned change in the installed operating model was found. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional factory lessons distillation and recall, memory, project configuration, cron triggers, coding benchmarks and feature extensibility. - Closure path: failed/completed task lessons can be recalled in later prompts; this changes operational context but does not select an external-and-future option and install a revised organizational capability under S4 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated lessons and new task prompts are retrospective operating memory, not a demonstrated strategic environmental scan/adaptation closure. - Evidence: lessons feature, distillation/recall, docs factory/bench boundary. - Basis: bounded negative-state structural review. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped mode. - Caveats: an external project owner can redesign/deploy the system; contributor development is outside this deployed recursion and does not imply a shipped S4 parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: a legitimate human project owner settles and changes the installed project&#39;s identity, membership authority and binding operating boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project/repository identity changes, member access conflicts and the limits under which assistant or factory agents may act. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update the project, change its repository/default branch and assign/revoke member roles; maintain at least one owner. - Decision owner: authenticated human project owner (and separately system admin for account approval), never the task agent or deterministic role checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner-only routes, persistent Project/Member store, role-based guards on API/supervisor operations, audit/feed, per-task policy and merge-permission enforcement. - Closure path: identity/membership/policy issue → project owner edits authoritative project/roles (or approves a new actor) → changes persist and are enforced on subsequent session access, supervisor acts, factory task/project use and integration permissions. - Boundary reachability: harness-web -team provides owner-gated project patch/member routes; membership changes immediately govern user and agent-action access in the same installed product. No upstream maintainer authority is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent may propose work and act with human approval, but ultimate project membership, repository identity and operating authority remain owner-governed; static policy checks enforce their decisions. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which repository constitutes the project and who may authorize/perform work on behalf of its organization. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the project owner in team mode; no autonomous ultimate-policy mode established for a single CLI agent. - Return-to-operation path: owner-only PATCH /projects/{pid} or role assignment → stored project/role update → subsequent requireProject and server/agent tool checks recognize the new authority and branch/repo context. - Evidence: owner-only project/role changes, team membership and access model, factory policy/merge modes. - Basis: structural parent-governed team mode. - Confidence: medium; explicit authorization/return path, not a claim about the upstream OSS organization&#39;s policy process. - Caveats: ordinary tool approval or a single PR merge veto alone would not justify S5; the claimed right is the project-identity/member-authority loop.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8278b99a5810ef26bcf4000cc6213cb41cbc4149/assessments/7a/7ar-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8278b99a5810ef26bcf4000cc6213cb41cbc4149\">8278b99 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-17T22:47:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Bowra/Bowra\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bowra</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-17\">2026-11-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Bowra/Bowra/tree/c6f444b112c57f686ef7d0b7a0c814663e667293\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c6f444b112c57f686ef7d0b7a0c814663e667293\">c6f444b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Conduct model-directed business/document analysis through the first-party ordinary Lead and bound specialist execution paths. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varied requests, ambiguous business metrics, missing document evidence, provider/tool returns and conflicting source claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead model decides which tools and specialist delegated tasks to use, asks questions or reworks an answer; specialist agents choose their task actions within grants. - Decision owner: First-party model-backed Lead, with subordinate specialist model executions in authorized workspace mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gateway, task/run limits, scoped tool registry, data and knowledge query, artifact identity, source references, sandbox and execution binding. - Closure path: User objective → model choices/delegated specialists → results with provenance → Lead synthesis/correction and delivery. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Community ordinary chat Lead, admitted subagentspawn and document/analysis skills with configured model/services. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM Lead makes substantive analytical and task decisions; deterministic source/revision/scope gates only constrain execution. - Evidence: Lead model orchestration; live continuation; Community capability boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for implemented model loops, conditional on configured Community runtime. - Caveats: No live business documents or data integrations were used; Community distribution must be configured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate a concrete inconsistency between specialists depending on disputed evidence, blocking questions or overlapping task scope, with a bounded first-party coordination construct. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different independently assigned specialist executions may need the same source/result or disagree about responsibility and scope, causing premature or inconsistent completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve or waive a required dependency and authorize a corrected answer/scope through the existing task owner before the specialist resumes. - Decision owner: A Lead or specialist can request, reply and revise via bound first-party agentmessage path, but autonomous conflict-resolution judgement across multiple independent units is not established as a complete standing strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Admitted responsibility cards, immutable execution IDs, authenticated artifact refs, ask/reply/update/resolve/wait ledger, durable checkpoint reauthorization. - Closure path: Specialist conflict or missing prerequisite → ask task owner with provenance → authorized answer / blocked/waived limitation → waiting specialist resumes with revised scope or records limitation. - Boundary reachability: First-party optional admitted collaboration is wired to live worker, ownership and same-Turn scope; unavailable conditions fall back rather than manufacturing completed coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Specific dependency and task-boundary arbitration tools/feedback are exposed, not merely a generic mailbox; still requires configured discretionary arbitration ownership for full autonomous S2, so C not A. - Distinct S1 units: Different specialist executions delegated from the ordinary Lead, each with exact execution IDs and separate assignments. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A dependent specialist cannot satisfy an assigned task without a bounded answer or evidence from another assigned worker; inconsistent hand-off or waived blockers risk unsupported completion. - Attenuating coordination relation: Admitted agentmessage ask/answer/resolve with persisted owner and immutable reference checks; unresolved dependencies become explicit limitations rather than false success. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Specialist waits and then consumes the returned authorization/evidence or records refusal; subsequent work is changed by resolved dependency. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The code implements a required dependency resolution/waiver protocol tied to real assigned work and evidence, with return-to-waiter semantics rather than unvalidated messages. - Evidence: dependency and owner ledger; runtime wait and return; Lead continuity. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; optional narrow construction path, not universal inter-agent orchestration. - Caveats: Only admitted same-Turn assigned participants; no generalized cross-project conflict resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Manage the current portfolio of one active Lead Turn&#39;s delegated specialist obligations and accepted results, with a distinct user-guided continuation mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist returns can be incomplete, blocked or out of scope; parallel task commitments must be tracked and reintegrated before final delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which specialists are needed, scope delegated work, inspect completed material, follow up on blockers and decide what the Lead can substantiate; in distinct parent mode the original user revises guidance mid-run. - Decision owner: Base A: model-backed Lead selecting current task delegation/synthesis. Parent P: legitimate initiating user through first-party mid-run revision/question interface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent execution IDs, responsibility cards, current-turn checkpoints, durable joins, model-tool budgets, revisions, artifact access controls. - Closure path: Lead-assigned obligations → specialist returns and authorized question replies → Lead resumes, adjusts delegated current work and final answer; user revisions are applied to next ongoing execution when scoped. - Boundary reachability: Lead orchestration and ordinaryleadruntime are installed in Community; optional user continuation engages only with live Team membership, worker lease and original chat Turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model Lead owns discretionary current assignments/return acceptance, not the raw scheduler; the parent mode requires active authenticated user revision returned to continuing Lead, not mere dashboard visibility. - Whole-system current view: Active single Turn&#39;s assigned helpers, exact responsibility cards, returned artifacts, unresolved requests and current execution budget. - Current-control decision scope: Task assignments, requirements, scope, waiting on relevant other specialists, follow-up and delivery of the current multi-agent request. - Evidence: model Lead and delegation; continuation and accepted returns; user steering and obligations. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; limited recursion one Turn, not entire multi-tenant SaaS. - Caveats: Do not generalize current Lead control to all Team users, sessions or repository governance.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No verified installed independent operational-reality audit of the ordinary Lead&#39;s primary claims returned to its current controlling loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A produced business answer may be inaccurate despite citations, and a model grader might be misled by the same text. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No claim-relative separate S3 audit judgement coupled back to the active Lead&#39;s real operational artifacts was established. - Decision owner: Not established at current Lead/Turn boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lead self-delivery review checks its own call IDs; separate Agent Eval model judge assesses answer text in version testing; explicit owner version review is about future release. - Closure path: Artifact checks and evaluation can reject draft/version candidates, but do not constitute complementary independent primary-observation audit of ordinary live operations with returned S3 intervention. - Boundary reachability: The named modules are first-party, but their supported purpose and event identity do not match this current-operation audit function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second model grading the same textual answer is not automatically complementary S3; deterministic source/effect validations are supporting first-party evidence checks, not a separate audit owner. - Evidence: answer-only evaluator; same-Turn delivery review; version tests. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium bounded negative. - Caveats: Future or independently configured audit could exist, but is not inferred from output safety/citations and benchmark runs.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Convert externally observed member/owner feedback and test evidence into prospective agent behavior/version improvements controlled by the legitimate Agent owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User feedback reveals that an existing agent&#39;s instructions omit periods, misinterpret causal data or otherwise perform poorly on new work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review Coach&#39;s proposed test and draft changes, accept required evidence and authorize future release of an improved Agent version. - Decision owner: Agent owner or team reviewer in first-party owner-approved mode; Coach model suggests changes and tests, but cannot unilaterally publish an authoritative new release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Coach question/point calibration, evidence-bounded working draft, before/after and held-out runs, candidate comparison, exact version/hashes, owner release checks and grants. - Closure path: External feedback/observed failures → Coach develops a prospective option → versioned draft and model/evidence assessment → owner accepts/reviews → released agent version used on subsequent tasks. - Boundary reachability: First-party Community Coach when available; owner workbench and local Developer API optional flags explicitly described. In unsupported deployments this mode is not claimed active. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed Coach proposes future adaptation, but the decisive launch into normal capability remains parent-owned and returns through explicit version release. Hence S4=P, not A. - External distinction: Owner/member complaint about an actual task result, held-out failures and changes in business requirements relative to the agent&#39;s prior behavior. - Future / prospective distinction: New prompt/tools/skills/tests target later Agent calls beyond fixing the single current answer. - Adaptation option generated: Coach-generated saved Agent draft or bounded change proposal plus a revised suite/evaluation case and before/after comparison. - Path back into current capability / S3: Approved released Agent version becomes available to subsequent ordinary project work, subject to the existing version/audience policy. - Evidence: Coach loop and held-out; Coach authoring; owner gate; release and review. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported parent loop; gated and optional. - Caveats: No real held-out numerical performance or active deployment was measured; source code says automation cannot publish itself.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No closed identity-level or ultimate-policy ratification loop at the one Lead Turn/project organization was evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Constitutional identity/mission conflicts differ from release approval and access control for a named Agent version. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Owner can set audience and authorize release, but no explicit system-purpose conflict hearing with returned ultimate identity decision was shown. - Decision owner: Team/Agent owner for configured access and release; S5 right not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team permissions, approval of Agent version and audience, identity/workspace ownership, secret and tool grants. - Closure path: Release or scope changes affect ordinary deployed version; they do not demonstrate adjudication of a foundational policy tension and return of a new ultimate policy. - Boundary reachability: Owner authorization and version governance are first-party, but the specific S5 function is not installed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Owner approval is a precondition to publishing an agent version and cannot simply be renamed ultimate policy. - Evidence: Agent release workflow; owner eligibility and version control; Dev API limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium bounded negative. - Caveats: Does not deny that private teams set durable organizational policies outside public source.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8ff5d1ef6de28e251aefff6629a4407e9306ddf0/assessments/bo/bowra.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8ff5d1ef6de28e251aefff6629a4407e9306ddf0\">8ff5d1e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:46:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AskTravel Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness/tree/9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\">9204095 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce researched trip advice and structured itineraries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: destinations, preferences, incomplete data, live tool observations and invalid itinerary arguments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose searches/playbooks, interpret observations and select/revise the itinerary. - Decision owner: the instantiated travelagent model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangChain execution, first-party tool wrappers, Postgres, retry/compaction middleware and Pydantic validation. - Closure path: tool and validation observations return to the model; presentitinerary persists and displays the chosen plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves storage/search wrappers without discretionary travel synthesis. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/api/openaicompat.py; src/travelagent/tools/itinerary.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: API lifespan constructs buildagent; the public chat router invokes that graph with user context.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No supplied regulation of a specific inter-S1 disturbance is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tools, independent chats and background upkeep must not be equated with coordinated autonomous work cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: thread/checkpoint branching, user namespaces, parallel tool execution and idle scheduling. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/threadsync.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-service current resource/commitment decision loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider rate limits and background workload are handled as configured runtime constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retry backoff, capped users per maintenance cycle, API state and database persistence. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/retry.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge the travel actor&#39;s persisted user-fact claims against raw user statements outside the ordinary chat-production path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misfiled interests/dislikes, contradicted or retracted saved facts and missing user-stated preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether a saved user claim needs add/update/delete/noop and propose the smallest source-supported correction. - Decision owner: the separately prompted structured memory-review model invoked by llmproposer; not applyplan&#39;s deterministic matcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle-chat discovery, checkpoint access, guarded store writes, source-quote checks, sensitive-fact exclusion, MAXOPS and memorylog. - Closure path: the reviewer proposes corrections; accepted operations update the shared fact store, and graph.systemprompt reloads that store on subsequent model calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the reviewer the scheduler and quote checker cannot decide semantic reclassification or contradiction; they only constrain/apply its proposed judgment. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; tests/testconsolidation.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A narrowly scoped source-supported memory-claim audit, not travel-answer verification. Same provider is permitted; trust is limited by selected recent messages, saved-fact evidence, model error, quote matching and excluded sensitive fields. No measured audit effectiveness. - Claim being audited: persisted user facts written by ordinary savememory calls faithfully represent user preferences/statements. - Ordinary reporting path: the travel actor writes summarized facts through savememory and later consumes their prompt projection. - Complementary access path: consolidateuser reads HumanMessage content directly from checkpoints and compares it to the store in a fresh structured review context. - Independence boundary: the producing chat actor neither selects the idle review invocation nor supplies its ordinary narrative as sole evidence; separate reviewer prompt and raw user messages can challenge that actor&#39;s stored claim. Shared provider/store do not establish global independence or coverage. - Who acts on findings: guarded applyplan updates memory and logs before/after; subsequent travel calls reread corrected facts. - Boundary reachability: app.lifespan creates the nonstreaming proposer and runconsolidationloop when default memoryconsolidation is enabled; disabled mode intentionally has no audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: live travel availability and changed user preferences are current-task/domain data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: search tools, skill playbooks, memory consolidation and dynamic prompt refresh. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/skills/init.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; docs/PLAN.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate-policy or identity decision-and-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task preferences and data controls are not identity-level organizational disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidelines, sensitive-memory guard, settings and user export/delete endpoints. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28/assessments/as/asktravel-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28\">5ae1eb5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:44:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenGenieMaster</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster/tree/8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\">8f6dab2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: primary embodied domain work (e.g. inspect/restock, retrieve, clean, handoff) by agent-guided robot work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task variation, unavailable/unknown objects, visual occlusion, robot capability limits, unexpected failures, interruptions and contradictory task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the in-process Claude coding agent chooses task decomposition, relevant tools, guidance, execution/recovery/handoff and whether to retry, based on live task context and returned observations. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent in the optional first-party ogm agent mode; individual robot policy owns lower-level execution, not the higher-level coding-agent choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, EEP schema, Harness, domain templates, adapters, admission interlocks, task store, completion criteria, typed failure/recovery hints, bounded turn count. - Closure path: goal → model tool decision → Harness.execute/robot → observation, checked outcome and persisted continuity → next model turn or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: documented ogm agent command constructs ClaudeAgent with first-party operator prompt and directly wired EEP tools; optional Anthropic SDK/API availability is an execution prerequisite. It is not necessary to invent an agent in application code. Offline fake-client tests demonstrate tool-loop wiring, not actual paid-model behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing ClaudeAgent leaves ScriptedOrchestrator, which can follow fixed recovery rules but does not retain the agent&#39;s open-ended choice of guidance and recovery under novel user contexts. S1 A belongs narrowly to the shipped optional Claude mode; the deterministic demo does not itself establish A. - Evidence: Claude tool loop, operator instructions, EEP handlers, execution/feedback, CLI agent entrypoint. - Basis: first-party optional agent tool loop with consequential environment feedback. - Confidence: high for code-level reachability, moderate for deployed agent outcomes (no provider E2E run). - Caveats: robot simulator is semantic; physical policy autonomy and real-world safety are not verified; provider model behaviour was not exercised.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen conflicts between distinct embodied S1 work cells over capacity, shared work-item affinity and interrupted handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately assigned robot/policy work cells with their own capability and physical state in a fleet; not two names for the same Claude agent. - Inter-S1 disturbance: assigning one busy robot simultaneously, transferring an unfinished pick–carry–place group while the first robot still holds its object, running dependent steps before prerequisites, or handing off to an incapable robot. - Attenuating coordination relation: Harness.dispatch excludes busy/stopped units, respects group affinity and dependencies; updatetask(assign/handoff) mediates exceptional reassignment. The agent&#39;s operating prompt directs failure-driven handoff and deliberate retry/wait/stop decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: assignment and handoff update persistent TaskStore; the next eeptaskget / eepdispatch / execution uses the new robot, group and dependency state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly detects the inter-robot work-item/capacity conflict and blocks/moves conflicting assignments, including held-object restart semantics; a simple shared task list would not do so. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent incompatible robot assignments and unstable cross-robot handoff in shared work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the autonomous Claude agent can choose to wait, hand off/reassign, force-restart affected work items, or route around failures based on robot feedback; deterministic dispatch enforces ordinary constraints but does not itself own discretionary S2 judgment. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent for exception coordination in the supported multi-robot mode; deterministic Harness for bound-preserving enforcement. Routine default dispatch alone would support only constructor/enforcement, not A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: busy-robot set, capability filtering, group binding, held-object tracking, dependency graph, continuity persistence, readiness checks. - Closure path: two robot work cells create assignment/holding conflict → feedback/continuity visible through EEP → agent chooses handoff/retry/alternate robot → guard updates whole work item → subsequent dispatch/execution observes new ownership. - Boundary reachability: multi-robot scenarios and eeptaskupdate handoff are first-party tools available directly to ClaudeAgent; no custom coordinator is required in that mode. S2 A does not claim the scripted dispatcher has autonomous organizational discretion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the full exception-response choice can be made by the tool-using model; deterministic locks/guards are supporting regulators. Removing the model would retain fixed handoff mechanics but not its context-sensitive coordination discretion. - Evidence: operating prompt, dispatch and busy rules, handoff safeguards, continuity group assignment, handoff regression. - Basis: identifiable multi-S1 interference, conflict-specific guard, model-accessible discretionary recovery and returned assignment state. - Confidence: moderate: source and synthetic test wiring are clear; real multi-robot model behavior is untested. - Caveats: if physical robots are only passive actuators under one central actor, the organizational S2 interpretation narrows; standard simulated policy work cells still retain local execution environments and separate assignments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: provide a current whole-fleet task/priority/resource regulation path, beyond local per-step delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous active tasks competing for finite robot availability, differing priorities, stopped robots and unmet commitments. - Whole-system current view: eeptasklist lists all tasks, eepcapabilities lists fleet skills/status, Harness.busyrobots spans active, paused and interrupted tasks, and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready enumerates all active tasks. - Current-control decision scope: assign resources across all ready tasks, use task priority when selecting the next work item, stop/pause or restart commitments and intervene on assignment. Task priority is chosen at creation, not autonomously renegotiated by a dedicated whole-system governor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a developer/composed operator or future first-party agent policy must decide whole-system priorities, commitments and exceptions; the shipped scheduler executes preselected priorities and capacity rules. - Decision owner: no autonomous agent demonstrably assigned sustained whole-system S3 discretion in the standard operating instructions. The caller chooses priorities and when the scheduler runs; scripts enforce them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: priority-sorted task store, cross-task dispatch, runready highest-priority selection, stop/resume and event log. - Closure path: all-task view + priorities → scheduled fleet assignment/intervention → persistent task/robot state → next scheduling pass. The first-party constructor is real; an autonomous whole-system deciding/feedback actor must still be composed. - Boundary reachability: eeptasklist, priority at eeptaskcreate, cross-task eepdispatch and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready are shipped callable modes. No private benchmark oracle is needed. This is a function-specific S3 path, not mere generic tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scripted runready orders work by caller-supplied priority; Claude&#39;s documented ogm agent goal prompt serves one task and does not establish an autonomous sustained fleet-wide current-control review. Enforcement is not ownership. Human eeptaskupdate calls are local operations, not a distinct operationally closed parent S3 mode. - Evidence: all-task and fleet view, task priorities and dispatch, cross-task busy guard, scheduler runready, tool registry. - Basis: shipped S3-specific whole-current-task capacity/priority constructor with missing autonomous decisive S3 policy. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: no operator-governed (P) whole-system governance closure, budget bargaining, or accountable strategic priorities is demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge the robot policy&#39;s own success report and the ordinary step-completion record through a separately callable, more direct evidence check. - Claim being audited: the physical action succeeded and its effect persists (e.g. an item really remains in the intended slot, not just policy-reported success). - Ordinary reporting path: robot adapter execute returns a policy success/outcome and observation; harness evaluates immediate criteria, records execution and updates task status. - Complementary access path: eepverify / recheckstep later calls criterion checks, optionally at close range, through the adapter&#39;s separate check operation; contradictory results mark prior execution records, convert succeeded steps to failed, and reopen completed tasks. - Independence boundary: the verification request and criterion signal are separate from the policy&#39;s initial self-reported success, and can overturn it in the shipped simulator. However physical HttpAdapter.check calls the same configured robot service (/check), with no guaranteed independent detector/operator/credential ownership; the model can also invoke the check itself. This is an explicit complementary constructor, not a demonstrated organizationally independent S3 auditor. - Who acts on findings: the harness reconciles continuity deterministically, and the coding agent can read discrepancies, recover or create new memory; a distinct audit owner/trigger/sampling authority is not supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive policy completion, persistent-effect regressions and false negatives hidden in routine execution summaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor&#39;s selection of when/how to check and corrective judgment is not operationally assigned; the first-party alternative-evidence and contradiction-to-rework feedback path is implemented. - Decision owner: needs first-party-mode integration/developer composition for independent S3 authority; Harness.recheckstep owns deterministic reconciliation, not independent audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: criteria API, alternate camera/close-range flag, contradiction records, task reopening and offline regression tests. - Closure path: policy claim → later direct criterion check → contradiction/reopening and marked evidence → S1 rework and future reflection. Distinct auditor authority is the missing component for A. - Boundary reachability: eepverify is exposed to the built-in Claude and MCP agents and Harness.recheckstep is shipped in the runtime; this is more than a test-only audit, though real detector independence is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a same-loop verifier can provide valuable extra evidence but does not prove a separately controlled independent audit; C recognizes the material constructor without fabricating A. - Evidence: recheck and task reopening, robot adapter direct check, tool eepverify, regression showing overturned completion, criteria. - Basis: separate criterion and corrective-return path, conditional independence at the evidence level, missing independent audit ownership for A. - Confidence: moderate; stronger simulation path than unverified real backend. - Caveats: same backend may correlate failures; normal immediate completion-criterion checks alone would not suffice for S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a capability-adaptation path using observed environmental difficulty and prospective targeted rehearsal, then return learned alternative guidance/coordination into subsequent work. - External distinction: repeated physical-world failures in particular robot/model/skill/object contexts, e.g. occluded targets, look-alike stock, changed item placements and slow handling, compared across execution records and domain catalog. - Future / prospective distinction: capability-gap severity and planned idle-time practice for troublesome contexts before the next customer/work task; this is an opportunity to develop/rehearse future response capacity, not just retry the current failed step. - Adaptation option generated: capabilitygaps groups recent failures, relative attempts and slowness; planpractice generates low-priority repeated pick/return tasks; RuleReflector or optional LLMReflector mines successful recoveries and contradictions into contextual patch instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: practice runs produce execution records → evidence-checked SharedAdmission admits or rejects guidance/recovery/criteria/coordination patches → GuidanceBuilder and chooserobot apply relevant memory on later dispatch/execution; weak non-safety memory can be retired based on outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: foreseeable performance deterioration or new capability demands in observed external object/robot contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: whether and when to run future-oriented practice, what capacity to spend, which gap to prioritize, and whether to make this a standing adaptation program. eepcapabilitygaps(schedulepractice=true) is callable, but no automatic idle/foresight owner and no sustained whole-system adaptation conversation with S3 are assigned in the standard runtime. - Decision owner: caller/developer must compose the autonomous S4 agenda and authority; deterministic gap ranking/practice creation and evidence-gated memory support do not by themselves own prospective choice. An optional Claude agent could call the constructor, but the shipped operator prompt lacks a recurring future-environment scan/idle planning loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution record history, contextual failure aggregation, low-priority practice workflow, rule/LLM reflection, schema/evidence/safety admission, versioned long-term memory and replay tests. - Closure path: environmental difficulty → exposed gap and practice option → caller-scheduled rehearsal → feedback/reflection/admitted patch → changed future guidance/robot preference. The function-specific construction path exists, but autonomous S4 initiative remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: eepcapabilitygaps with schedulepractice and memory tools are actually in the shipped tool registry, not just a README roadmap; rule reflection and admitted patches run in standard simulation and the MCP mode. Physical extrapolation not claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reflective memory loop alone is not S4. The distinct prospective practice option and future capability return make this more than generic memory, while lack of a scheduled autonomous environmental/adaptation governor keeps it at C. - Evidence: capability-gap/practice construction, tool exposure, reflection, admission safety/evidence, memory application, architecture memory path. - Basis: first-party prospective gap/practice-to-later-capability constructor; no complete autonomous S4 decision loop or parent mode. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: gaps are mined from internal execution records of externally situated work, not broad market/strategy sensing. There is no robot-weight self-training; flywheel.py stops at dataset export.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: would decide or revise ultimate identity/mission and balance current robot operations against the future adaptation program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved mission/safety/strategic-policy tensions beyond routine robot-task control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime path accepts a genuinely ultimate-policy question, routes it to legitimate authority, records an authoritative identity decision and returns it to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: none evidenced for an active S5 loop; hard-coded safety constraints and user task goals do not constitute an identity governor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: domain safety rules, SharedAdmission rejection of unsafe guidance/memory, robot stop/resume, current-model prompt, human teleoperation example, repo contributor governance. - Closure path: not established at ultimate-policy level; operational stop/resume and evidence-based memory patches affect tasks or safety settings rather than deciding identity or arbitrating S3–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent cannot revise ultimate mission under a dedicated first-party process, and no first-party parent institution/human S5 escalation-return path is supplied. Accepting task goals and protecting immutable safety flags are ordinary operating constraints. - Evidence: operator prompt/safety, admission rules, memory admission cannot weaken safety, robot stop/resume, human backend, research vs repo identity note. - Basis: bounded negative conclusion for reviewed runtime rather than universal impossibility. - Confidence: moderate-high within shipped modes. - Caveats: a real deployment may have human/organizational identity governance outside this repository, but that external authority cannot be inferred here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3/assessments/op/opengeniemaster.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3\">1c02129 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:37:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uniac-ai/AgentOrg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentOrg</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uniac-ai/AgentOrg/tree/25e116cb719989ce568b272016b1936668efbf5f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 25e116cb719989ce568b272016b1936668efbf5f\">25e116c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: attached autonomous agent sessions carry out locally variable project work and return artifacts/results as Work submissions within their declared position. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project requirements, source/tool results, work feedback and manager requests that demand operational judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select task actions and revise work within the Actor&#39;s purpose, then submit a result and respond to returned review or ask answers. - Decision owner: attached Claude Code/Codex or other autonomous agent session in the documented first-party MCP operating mode; operator supplies initial position/credentials and task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hub Actor registry, MCP adapter, Workspace records, audit store and session hooks, Provider provisioning (optional). - Closure path: agent receives assigned Work/attention, acts in its own project runtime, records progress and submits; requester reviews or changes assignment, after which the session receives and acts on returned state. - Boundary reachability: the distributed MCP adapter and bundled skill explicitly attach supported agent sessions and supply the Work/attention/submit path. This is a wired operating mode, not merely an open-ended API a developer might someday connect. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: cognition and project actions are in the attached external agent, credited as an actor only because the installed mode explicitly wires its decision/action/report loop; Hub persistence alone is not S1 autonomy. - Evidence: MCP agent client/attachment, Work bindings, agent Work protocol. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Hub alone creates no model session and cannot guarantee external agent behavior; no paid provider runtime was executed in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent simultaneous operational Actors from destructively claiming the same shared resource while they do distinct Work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two active S1 positions can contend for one release lane, cluster or other shared named resource. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller seeks/renews/releases a resource claim; Hub refuses another active holder and names the conflict; an agent must compose its own response, negotiation and later retry. - Decision owner: the holder/contender Actor selects a requested resource and intended follow-up; deterministic Hub claims enforce exclusive holding and expiry, but the standard distribution does not install an autonomous conflict-resolution policy for all Actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite claim store, lifetime/holder checks, authenticated Actor identity, conflict refusals and root override. - Closure path: the second claim returns a named-holder refusal; a custom agent policy or manager can subsequently wait, negotiate, change its work or retry after release/expiry, using the first-party claim path. - Boundary reachability: named claims are an explicit shipped Hub Tool available to attached Actors and the root, and the skill documents their shared-resource purpose. The autonomy/coordination response after refusal requires composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concrete exclusive-claim regulation is first-party and function-specific, but its mandatory refusal/expiry judgment is deterministic and no universal autonomous coordination-closure actor is installed. This warrants constructor C rather than A. - Distinct S1 units: separately attached active agent positions in one Hub organization, each with its own Work and local task environment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing claims for the same named shared lane or resource. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive holder check with conflict identity and explicit release/expiry. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: refusal tells the contender who holds the resource; custom agent/operator behavior can adapt task choice or retry once the claim ends. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: claims.ts enforces a specific shared-resource collision and returns a conflict, rather than merely transferring messages or exposing untyped storage. - Evidence: claim implementation, domain Tool catalog, bundled skill organization commands. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no mandatory agent-side claim discipline or automatic release notification/negotiation is established; the Hub&#39;s isolated conflict decision is not by itself evidence of agent S2 ownership.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current organization-wide or managerial-subtree commitments by assigning Work, responding to subordinate asks and accepting or returning submitted results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: reports may block, diverge from assigned work, need approvals or consume commitments that must be reviewed and reprioritized by a responsible manager. - Decisive decision or feedback right: see current direct-report Work/asks, choose ownership and goals, answer/escalate actionable asks, review a specific submission and return changes or accept it. - Decision owner: in the agent-manager mode, the attached manager&#39;s agent exercises this discretionary current-control right; in the distinct root CLI mode, the legitimate human root can make equivalent authoritative choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: team, actorview, Work and Forms records, role checks, attention/waiter delivery and stored revisions. - Closure path: manager observes report/ask/submission, chooses answer/reassignment or workreview verdict, Hub stores it, and the subordinate receives attention and updates/resubmits Work. Human root mode uses the same return path. - Boundary reachability: the standard-distribution agent skill directly instructs managers to inspect team state, assign/review subordinate Work and handle asks; the shipped root CLI can act as the canonical human root with the Hub&#39;s authenticated rights. Both modes use the same installed Work/attention path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in manager-agent mode, the agent selects which current intervention to make; deterministic record/authority checks enforce it. In root mode, the human is the decision owner. These are alternative modes, not concurrent owners of one decision. - Whole-system current view: manager&#39;s team and actorview show its reports&#39; Work state, current asks and review queue; canonical root can see the whole organization. - Current-control decision scope: assigning Work goals/owners, reviewing submitted commitments, answering/escalating blocking asks and changing subsequent report activity. - Evidence: team/attention projections, Workspace operation bindings, agent manager procedure, root CLI authority. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: not every connected Actor is a manager; an agent-specific actual review choice was not exercised with a provider. Human root control is specifically current-work supervision, not an S5 identity-policy claim.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped independent complementary inspection of operational reality beyond ordinary work reporting and manager reviews is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers can misreport completion, but a separate audit-specific counter-observation path is not provided by the Hub. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent raw-artifact audit judgment with corrective return is established. - Decision owner: managers review submissions through ordinary Work state; the root reads operation metadata, neither becomes a distinct packaged S3 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only activity metadata, Work review verdicts, status/attention, Hub audit events and client context. - Closure path: review changes can return to a worker as S3 accountability, not an independently sourced complementary operational audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root activity read observes tool-call metadata, while workreview is normal reporting/review; no separately deployed direct-evidence auditor is wired. - Evidence: activity and Work boundaries, audit metadata code, manager skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a connected organization&#39;s external agents can run tests or review code, but that independently configured audit is not a default Hub function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environment/option-generation and return-to-current-capability loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external demand and capability changes may affect the organization but are not converted by a shipped S4 process into adaptation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct future-oriented model, option selection and S3 return path. - Decision owner: an agent/user may propose new work, but the Hub has no S4-specific owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Work backlog, reports/asks, status, provider catalog, generic messages and stored historical activity. - Closure path: no shipped external-prospecting option-to-implementation path; Work assignments remain present-task control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: storage and future-dated Work do not show externally grounded environmental modeling or adaptation. - Evidence: Tool catalog and projections, agent skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: organization-specific skills might implement prospective assessment; they are external to this reviewed standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the Hub models organization membership/purpose and enforces role authority, but no first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: creating/closing a position, credential revocation and action approvals are governed structural operations, not necessarily policy/identity tensions of the whole organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct workflow takes an ultimate-policy issue to legitimate authority, decides organizational ethos/policy and returns an enforceable new policy into operation. - Decision owner: human root chooses membership and routine configuration; agent managers can create authorized reports. Neither is evidenced as an S5-specific decision path merely from hierarchical titles or rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Actor registry, credentials, lifecycle checks, hierarchy, approval Forms and filtered interfaces. - Closure path: Actor create/close and approval answers change ordinary access/work; absent is an explicit identity-policy issue/decision/returned governance process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root actor and purpose fields do not themselves prove the VSM S5 function; generic Forms allow many applications but do not compose ultimate policy. - Evidence: registry/lifecycle, Hub design authority, agent skill approval limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a particular organization may implement genuine parent S5 over these generic tools, but that case requires its own first-party operating contract and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/448a5f4f4273aa7aaa5175f4e71e491562962a2d/assessments/ag/agentorg.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 448a5f4f4273aa7aaa5175f4e71e491562962a2d\">448a5f4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-16T18:51:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halecraft/pinetrie\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pine·trie</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-16\">2026-11-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halecraft/pinetrie/tree/0f4483f4aa86058347448f616848b475cb8c0fa5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0f4483f4aa86058347448f616848b475cb8c0fa5\">0f4483f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out coding or inquiry tasks on a conversational thread. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different jobs, tool outcomes and changes in the lane&#39;s working copy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tool actions from thread context and use their returned results to continue or answer. - Decision owner: The installed lane&#39;s model-backed agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: A Pi-backed harness instance, thread-specific tool context, containerized operations, Kyneta session and turn recording. - Closure path: The tool&#39;s response reaches the lane&#39;s agent continuation. At turn boundaries, file effects and primary messages are recorded and visible as proposed work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides which permitted tools to use and how to respond. The host supplies lane storage, tool registration and dispatch. - Evidence: packages/pi/agent.ts, packages/pi/tools.ts, ARCHITECTURE.md, README.md. - Basis: First-party model/tool binding, continuation and turn records. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Pi and the model provider are dependencies. The cited lane, tools, history and feedback are wired by the product. - Boundary reachability: The shipped host builds the Pi-backed agent with its own tool registration, thread-specific context and turn-recording hooks, and starts it in the thread lane.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Resolve dependencies or disagreements among active operational threads. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Separate thread agents can reach incompatible answers or require one another&#39;s results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide if a peer is to answer a discrepancy, select that peer, and formulate the returned task. - Decision owner: The model the host invokes with its bundled agents-round skill. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Completed peer answer gathering, member-constrained reply parser, allowance accounting and actual relay. - Closure path: The host observes a completed multi-agent round, asks the model whether to intervene, parses its choice and delivers a new ask to the selected thread agent. The answer can resolve the identified dependency or disagreement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model picks the live follow-up target and question. The host validates membership and enforces budget and relay. - Evidence: apps/pinetrie/host/answers.ts, packages/pi/skills/agents-round/SKILL.md, packages/rules/told.ts, apps/pinetrie/host/work/update.ts. - Basis: Shipped follow-up wiring plus a separate lane conflict mechanism. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The decisive example is an eligible person-triggered multi-agent round. Lane isolation and catch-up also attenuate file conflicts. - Boundary reachability: The run-end handler dispatches the bundled round skill after eligible replies; its structured output drives a real follow-up relay. - Distinct S1 units: Peer thread agents with distinct contexts and lane work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Disagreeing answers or dependencies between peer-agent results on a shared request. - Attenuating coordination relation: A model reviews the replies together and can task one identified peer with a specific reply to resolve an open disagreement or dependency. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The validated ask is delivered to the selected thread and becomes a new request for that agent. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The follow-up decision is conditional on a completed multi-agent round and explicit disagreement or dependence. Its output changes a peer&#39;s subsequent activity.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Choose the currently accepted project basis among alternative lane contributions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent agent and human proposals can conflict or be based on stale accepted work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which prepared revision or stack advances the shared accepted baseline, or leave it pending. - Decision owner: The project operator through the acceptance workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cross-thread work summaries, diff inspection, accepted bookmark, destination and conflict checks, serialized writes. - Closure path: The admitted revision advances accepted work. Other threads receive a base-change signal and can update their actual work to the changed basis. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents propose and can repair their lane work. The user owns advancement of accepted shared work and its destinations. - Evidence: docs/pinetrie.md, apps/pinetrie/host/jj/proposed/act.ts, apps/pinetrie/host/folder/writes.ts, apps/pinetrie/host/work/update.ts. - Basis: First-party operator surface, proposal acceptance rules and shared base propagation. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The evidence covers project baseline control. A newer base reaches existing lanes when they update; it does not silently merge into running work. - Boundary reachability: The installed host exposes proposal and accept commands for both jj and folder projects, and its lane machinery reads the accepted baseline. - Whole-system current view: A project-wide tree of active lanes, their proposed changes and their conflicts against current accepted work, with material diffs available for the user&#39;s choice. - Current-control decision scope: Current admission and destination of proposed project changes into the authoritative baseline used by peer lanes.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Audit material operational claims independently of normal proposal reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A free-form report can disagree with the work&#39;s actual contents or outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect operational reality with an audit mandate independent of ordinary production/acceptance and return a correction to current control. - Decision owner: No function-specific first-party audit owner identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Proposal summaries, separate file diff reads for the acceptance decision, conflict detection and peer asks. - Closure path: The data feeds ordinary acceptance and source synchronization. Peer answers inform coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Diff reading supplies primary evidence to the person who accepts a proposal. No distinct auditor is tasked to verify agents&#39; operational claims and return an audit verdict. - Evidence: apps/pinetrie/host/folder/review.ts, docs/pinetrie.md, apps/pinetrie/host/answers.ts, packages/pi/skills/agents-round/SKILL.md. - Basis: Review reads, proposal acceptance and built-in peer skills. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: A project can request independent ad hoc review through a separate thread. That judgment is not an installed audit loop in the reviewed mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Adopt reusable model, command, skill or prompt changes for future project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future workflows can require model capabilities or tools beyond the current host setup. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which proposed project setup change becomes a durable future operating capability. - Decision owner: The project operator accepting the configuration change. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Supported project configuration layers, lane proposals, config-change feedback and host Pi startup setup. - Closure path: The operator accepts a revision with updated setup, accepted configuration is read in the next host startup, and its models or reusable skills become part of later thread operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents can propose the change. Authority to make a lane&#39;s setup proposal the project&#39;s future setup remains with the operator. - Evidence: apps/pinetrie/host/work/config.ts, apps/pinetrie/host/TECHNICAL.md, docs/pinetrie.md, apps/pinetrie/host/jj/proposed/act.ts. - Basis: Source-wired accepted configuration and documented host startup semantics. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: This covers setup adoption. Some changes require an operator restart and the assessment makes no claim of an autonomous environmental scan. - Boundary reachability: The shipped startup reads accepted work&#39;s configuration and passes resolved models, skills and prompts to the Pi setup. The shipped accept operation advances the input revision. - External distinction: A project operator can choose models, supported external skill packages or new runbooks to address upcoming work outside the current thread. - Future / prospective distinction: Project setup changes deliberately persist beyond the producing turn and can govern future host launches. - Adaptation option generated: A proposed revision of project configuration choosing a new reusable skill, prompt or model capability. - Path back into current capability / S3: Acceptance advances the source for startup setup. The next host reads that accepted state and runs future lanes with the adopted capability.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set the standing authority of lanes to reach project-shared resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A proposed scope change can introduce new directories or access grants that would change the project&#39;s operating boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authorize the project-level grants by deciding which configuration revision becomes accepted. - Decision owner: The project operator who can advance the accepted project basis. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Layered authority decoding, accepted config preflight, share/grant application and lane start specification. - Closure path: The accepted base changes, the host rereads accepted authority, applies the sharing/grant plan then takes the new config into force. Lanes use the accepted authority in subsequent starts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents can make proposals inside their lane scope. The host enforces accepted project authority and the person chooses the binding project grant as part of acceptance. - Evidence: apps/pinetrie/host/work/config.ts, apps/pinetrie/host/work/setup.ts, apps/pinetrie/host/work/setup.ts, docs/pinetrie.md. - Basis: First-party config and share-and-grant application. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Scope is project-level lane rights. User and host settings may further constrain the effective permissions. - Boundary reachability: The installed host&#39;s lane setup guards access through the accepted-config and share-and-grant path, which is re-read when accepted work changes. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which external directories/resources are within the continuing lane operating and trust boundary. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The project operator choosing accepted configuration within host/user-level limits. - Return-to-operation path: An accepted change is decoded, host share-and-grant is applied, accepted configuration is replaced and subsequent lane specs reflect the choice.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/911b9c49fb285e4632265fafaee3c6e58fb0547f/assessments/pi/pine-trie.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 911b9c49fb285e4632265fafaee3c6e58fb0547f\">911b9c4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T22:41:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ShuaiMXu/happyhands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HappyHands</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ShuaiMXu/happyhands/tree/d469a02cb9b98c5f1a85b05201f5401bbec20ebf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d469a02cb9b98c5f1a85b05201f5401bbec20ebf\">d469a02 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Implement a scoped product-experience repair or page composition in the isolated target. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different UI defects, source conditions and verification evidence require task-specific implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose how to reproduce a finding, whether it is actionable, and which bounded source edits resolve it. - Decision owner: The configured implementation agent reached through the shipped runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source-bound plan, selected finding set, worktree, execution limit, captured originals and structural checks. - Closure path: The Workbench creates or reads a confirmed scope, invokes the runner, captures the changed preview and records checks and the result for review. - Boundary reachability: The automatic repair action and the CLI execute command both reach the bundled isolated runner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the agent the harness can capture and validate, but it cannot make the supported task-specific implementation decisions. - Evidence: docs/harness.md, docs/harness.md, packages/workbench/service.js, packages/harness-core/runner.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Repair authority is user-delegated. Final QA and acceptance are separate decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Regulate interference among multiple independent S1 production units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent product edit work or shared component edits could interfere if the harness supplied multiple coequal workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No material inter-S1 coordination right is shipped in the assessed product loop. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run identity, per-run action and stage locks, isolated worktrees and job capacity. - Closure path: The Workbench serializes incompatible stages in a single run and isolates separate executions. Review batches return QA evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operating chain uses one implementation worker per repair. Reviewers and deterministic checks are different organizational roles, and the inspected job controls supply no decision path for interference among distinct S1 units. - Evidence: packages/workbench/service.js, packages/workbench/direction-execution.js, docs/architecture.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate the active review and repair commitment from source-bound evidence and verification state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale or false findings, insufficient evidence, failed repair and revised scope can invalidate the current commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine which scoped work may run, and on failed QA decide whether to reject or direct a fresh evidence-bound review. - Decision owner: The product operator using the Workbench or CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run-wide state, source binding, per-stage gates, explicit repair requests, a retained worktree and judgment records. - Closure path: The operator chooses a repair scope or a new evidence-backed review and the service binds that choice to the plan or review. Rejection ends that run and acceptance requires a separate QA pass. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Workbench actions and CLI tools expose source review, execution authorization and result control over the same ledger. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The implementation agent judges the edits inside its remit. The operator owns intervention on the current commitment and the disposition of the result. - Whole-system current view: For the assessed product run the ledger and workbench aggregate baseline, findings, plan, worktree result, after evidence, QA and judgment. - Current-control decision scope: Scope the active repair, reject the result or return it for evidence-bound review before the next decision. - Evidence: docs/harness.md, docs/harness.md, packages/workbench/service.js, packages/harness-core/workflow.js. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently audit whether the change achieves the user task without material visual regression. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The executor&#39;s report and deterministic layout checks can miss actual experience regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Issue a visual verdict from directly captured, source-bound before and after evidence. - Decision owner: The separate configured Visual QA model session in the shipped model-review mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable originals, coverage and state binding, checks, separate review context and judgment gate. - Closure path: A failing or incomplete review blocks acceptance. New evidence can trigger a fresh review, whose result governs the next operator disposition. - Boundary reachability: The Workbench review path invokes the shipped reviewer after recapture and structural evaluation. The lightweight CLI also exposes model review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The independent reviewer reasons over direct evidence and reports findings, while the runtime checks provenance and enforces gates. - Claim being audited: The repaired user experience meets the task without unacceptable visual regression. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementation result, changed files, executor claims and structural evaluation. - Complementary access path: Matched original browser captures and bound specialist evidence in a separate review session. - Independence boundary: Visual QA is produced separately from the implementation session and checks the actual changed render. - Who acts on findings: The workflow blocks acceptance and the product operator can reject or direct new evidence and independent re-review. - Evidence: docs/experience-orchestration.md, packages/workbench/ai-review.js, packages/workbench/ai-review.js, packages/harness-core/workflow.js. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Develop and return a prospective adaptation of the harness&#39;s future product-review capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed product needs and repeated visual failures may motivate new method choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a reviewed adaptation and make it affect future operation. - Decision owner: The operator can admit cases and approve durable design changes. A maintainer can publish and activate Expertise metadata. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source-bound case admissions, future-plan retrieval, independent regression reports and versioned release pointers. - Closure path: Admitted cases can enter later plans as references. Expertise activation changes a release pointer, but the inspected operational runner did not establish that the pointer changes the loaded capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The concrete case path changes context for later work. The inspected release path has a gap between recording activation and changing operational method selection. - Evidence: docs/experience-cases.md, docs/project-memory-lifecycle-2026-10-18.md, packages/skill-core/releases.js, packages/skill-core/releases.js. - Basis: structural -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set the durable design and experience rules that govern future product work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Observed or proposed visual conventions may conflict with the product&#39;s intended identity and standards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide whether proposed brand, design and reusable rules are approved, replaced or deprecated as the authoritative Foundation. - Decision owner: The human product owner/operator authorized to approve the product&#39;s Foundation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Observed inventory, explicit reasoned decisions, version binding and plans constrained by the approved snapshot. - Closure path: The extracted or staged proposal reaches the operator, whose approval writes an immutable Foundation and updates the Experience Spec. Bootstrap plans and execution load and check the same approved version. - Boundary reachability: The shipped workbench and CLI expose Foundation approval, with a ledger gate and a subsequent runner check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Extraction and generation implement or propose design choices. The human decides what visual language is authoritative for the product. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which visual language, design principles, brand voice and reusable design rules future product work must preserve. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The product operator acting for the product through explicit Foundation approval. - Return-to-operation path: The approved version becomes a prerequisite and input to first-render planning and execution. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md, docs/harness.md, packages/harness-core/store.js, packages/harness-core/runner.js. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/75bb11fb29027ed579e4d24016a815b3d95c710c/assessments/ha/happyhands.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 75bb11fb29027ed579e4d24016a815b3d95c710c\">75bb11f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:02:10+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pydromon</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon/tree/65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\">6567167 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configured session lines produce project work and exchange results through their first-party daemon composition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User tasks, local workspace changes, messages, background runs and engine/tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose local tools, communications, work actions and whether to continue or end a turn. - Decision owner: The model-backed session-line actor; external Claude/Codex/Pi engines supply inference/tool-loop dependencies rather than autonomous Dromon metasystem judgments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party driver construction, tool exposure, mailbox/event log, wake scheduler, sandbox/settings composition and persisted engine sessions. - Closure path: Queued mail/wake → line prompt and configured engine → chosen tools/messages/work → recorded turn outcomes → later line turns. - Boundary reachability: The distributed daemon creates configured session lines with first-party Dromon tools and driver/engine wiring. The supported Claude/Codex-backed composition is executable without implementing a new harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model-backed line actors, the daemon transports messages and enforces policy but cannot choose meaningful project work. Dromon does not receive credit for unrelated engine features. - Evidence: [Scheduler][R1], [engine composition][R2], [daemon][R3], [line configuration][R4], [README][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No live subscribed engine or paid model was run; engine independence and operational quality are not claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Declared resource leases and role-seat ownership regulate concrete shared-resource/write collisions among operational lines. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two active lines may try to use the same named project resource; role seats sharing a worktree must avoid competing writers and stale ownership transitions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A line chooses when to acquire/release a resource lease and can hand off or recover role ownership subject to observed eligibility. - Decision owner: Configured agent lines own invocation/release/handoff choices; the operator declares permitted resource names/role terms, and deterministic state validates exclusivity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lease holder/expiry events, refusal with holder/expiration feedback, non-renewal/TTL, role owner invariants, engine access rebuilding and journaled handoff. - Closure path: Contender calls acquire → receives grant or current-holder/expiry refusal → adjusts later resource use; release/expiry enables another acquisition. Role handoff records a note, transfers authority, wakes the successor and rebuilds the former owner&#39;s access. - Boundary reachability: acquirelease/releaselease and seat ownership tools are supplied to running lines by Scheduler; project-config leases/roles enable the shipped path, not a custom conflict resolver. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actors decide when to reserve/release/pass work; the log and guards enforce those decisions. This credits that bounded agent-mediated coordination mode, not generic messaging or the scheduler itself. - Evidence: [Lease tool and role tool implementations][R1], [role invariants/one-writer boundary][R6], [project enablement][R7], [line tool semantics][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Resource leases are cooperative: unmanaged external processes or ignored lease conventions are not forcibly excluded. Deterministic automatic failover is support, not autonomous discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: The project operator regulates the whole current set of session lines, role capacity, budgets and continuing commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Runaway autonomous turns/cost, held or failed lines, exhausted provider plans, conflicting current staffing and engine availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review project-wide state and choose line/role/budget/hibernation/rebind/interruption/release changes or answer a held line. - Decision owner: The authenticated local project operator, acting as the legitimate parent of the project run. No first-party autonomous whole-project controller is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Private operator socket, all-line status summaries, durable events, operator-attributed messages, hold notices/question queue, CLI commands and scheduler/engine enforcement. - Closure path: Whole-project status or held-line notice → operator current-control choice → daemon command/event → scheduler applies limits, ownership, model, hold or interruption changes → subsequent turns run under the returned decision. - Boundary reachability: Daemon.execute serves operator-authority status/settings/role/budget/configure/release/interrupt commands, and status includes every line. The first-party scheduler routes hold questions and operator answers back into later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the operator leaves fixed limits and failover machinery, not discretionary whole-system resource/commitment revision. P describes the supplied supervised mode; enforcement does not become A or C. - Evidence: [Operator protocol dispatch/current status][R3], [holds and operator-answer scheduler][R1], [project limits/roles][R7], [role runtime updates][R6], [client protocol][R8]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The parent boundary is this local project, not the public OSS organization; operator socket access is an explicit trust assumption.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No installed complementary independent audit judgment-and-corrective-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect work-completion claims, stale notes, disclosure mistakes and engine-reporting errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate audit-specific actor or constructor adjudicates operational truth and returns corrective control. - Decision owner: Lines report their work; runtime observes process/session/log facts. Read-only seats are configured by users without a supplied audit workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon-stamped commit hashes, role journals, activity/event logs, raw transport evidence/replay and disclosure checks. - Closure path: Facts can be inspected by an operator or peer; the standard product has no audit verdict/corrective-action protocol for a particular completion claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent fact capture is useful evidence but is not itself discretionary complementary audit; tests, developer reviews and optional advisory seats do not supply an installed audit function. - Evidence: [Scheduler status/journal/check tools][R1], [engine transport evidence][R2], [evidence store][R9], [role design][R6], [line tools][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A user can organize reviewer lines through general messages; that application would need separate function/independence/closure evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider budget changes, new model choices and suggested project patches are available current inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped intelligence actor develops future-oriented capability options and closes their return into current capability. - Decision owner: Operator chooses reconfiguration/upgrades; ordinary lines may submit untrusted patch proposals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proposechange storage, project reload/rebind, provider-budget failover and optional local teacher lifecycle. - Closure path: Proposals are explicitly never applied by Dromon; teacher code only loads/unloads a local model. Rebinding/failover responds to present availability rather than prospective adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A future roadmap, a model called teacher, context handoff or a current capacity switch is not S4. Generic operator patch handling does not create a function-specific parent adaptation loop. - Evidence: [Proposal no-apply contract][R10], [teacher boundary][R11], [role failover][R6], [reload/rebind][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Repository-development/dogfood decisions and future Rust/Keleusma ports are adjacent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No function-specific identity/ultimate-policy decision-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access/disclosure rules, operator messages, project instructions and role terms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is specifically adjudicated and returned by ultimate authority as a new governing policy. - Decision owner: Operator configures the project and its permission/disclosure boundaries; agents have explicitly bounded local tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Operator/line address attribution, private socket trust, disclosure checks, tool/recipient policies, protected state and role rules. - Closure path: Configuration and operator answers alter current execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue/decision protocol or S3–S4 balance closure is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator is a real parent for S3 current control; that does not promote all configuration or question replies to S5. - Evidence: [Operator and line authority split][R3], [line tools/policy][R4], [project configuration][R7], [scheduler trust and question semantics][R1]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: First-party development documents record human design decisions, but those are not an installed policy actor in an arbitrary user project.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db/assessments/py/pydromon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db\">b8dbf77 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:09:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">INIT-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent/tree/1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\">1b8b407 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Convert coding requests into tool-mediated work, revise actions after observed outcomes and return results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code/task state, model results, tool failures, user steering and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select subsequent code/tool actions and responses from the model&#39;s observations. - Decision owner: Mutable agent-body model in the normal supported session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model I/O types, tool dispatcher, context/log, kernel process lifecycle, Root sandbox and egress proxy. - Closure path: Input/context → model-selected tool call → dispatched tool result in the conversation → next model decision. - Boundary reachability: The installed init-agent launches the shipped body loop through the kernel; tool use is not borrowed from the provider&#39;s internal agent service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party model chooses substantive tool use and adjusts after feedback; Root only constrains execution and supplies transport. - Evidence: seed loop, model tool-call types, tool dispatcher, body boot. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection only; no paid provider or installed-session E2E execution was performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately evidenced first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation loop at this chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential interference among multiple independent S1 operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific inter-S1 conflict-resolution right established. - Decision owner: None evidenced for S2 in the normal distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded delegation, serial body turn execution, process/sandbox separation and ordinary result return. - Closure path: Delegation and result reporting support S1 task decomposition, without an evidenced S2-specific coordination correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple processes and a delegate tool do not independently demonstrate an interference/oscillation witness and subsequent coordination feedback. - Evidence: delegate implementation, delegate tool, system architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This does not assert that a custom future fleet cannot implement S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent-controlled current recovery and runtime constraint changes over the one live agent-and-Root system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe operation, health regressions, continued erroneous work, spend/exposure concerns and failed self-changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect system status and select owner panic/stop/recovery/rollback rather than simply accept the current run. - Decision owner: Legitimate human operator via the Root&#39;s owner channel; Root executes the returned choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root status snapshot, owner socket/CLI, signed lineage, sandbox, watchdog and snapshot restore. - Closure path: Root current status and warnings → owner chooses panic, stop, resume or rollback → Root changes currently permitted execution/recovered tuple. - Boundary reachability: init-root status, attach, panic, resume and Root recovery are supported installed owner commands, not contributor CI or a suggested external management plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent selects consequential whole-system intervention; deterministic Root enforcement and autonomous threshold rollback alone are not credited as agent-owned S3 discretion. - Whole-system current view: Root publishes live slot/mode/level/charter, process state, spend, snapshots and fault/lineage status for the deployed system. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or resume current work and restore/restrict the active body state and access envelope. - Evidence: Root owner CLI, Root contract, README controls. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Parent path is local owner control, not an autonomous multi-agent S3 or organizational governance of repository contributors.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: A Root-sealed, agent-inaccessible regression check of evolved bodies is present; its ownership as the Methodology&#39;s discretionary complementary S3 audit remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported successful mutation concealing functional regression or benchmark gaming. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Challenge the current body with unseen tasks and return regression/rollback decisions. - Decision owner: Sealed deterministic Root evaluator executes tests and thresholds; no separately evidenced autonomous discretionary audit judge is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root-only sealed task store, canaries, paired comparisons, noise floors, signed audit records and rollback mechanism. - Closure path: Sealed task results → deterministic regression verdict → rollback/demotion or accepted audited pointer, affecting subsequent body operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Complementary first-party access and correction exist, but the recorded judge is deterministic enforcement. Further bounded analysis is needed to decide whether the established function&#39;s published ownership is C, — or unresolved under Methodology 0.3.7. - Evidence: Root sealed evaluation contract, Root module wiring, promotion gate contract. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: low for ownership, high for shipped sealed infrastructure. - Caveats: This is not an independent reviewer run or proof of successful model performance.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Propose and deploy changes to the agent&#39;s future operating capabilities from observed task/tool limitations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing requirements, repeated tool failures, inefficiencies and capability mismatch with prospective work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses edits to its own prompt, extension/tool set or compiled body and requests their promotion. - Decision owner: Agent model authors the adaptation option; Root/kernel safety and performance gates may reject it without owning option generation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable body/extension worktree, initctl promote, G0–G4 tests, staged process hot-swap, lineage and safety rollback. - Closure path: Observed limitations → agent-authored source/extension change → promotion/gates → accepted live body/tool capability used on later turns; rejected change returns evidence for another attempt. - Boundary reachability: Self-edit is a documented native agent capability; released kernel/Root perform the promotion and can preserve conversation state through a core swap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The source modification and future option are model-selected; a fixed compiler/test/holdout guard has veto power but does not independently choose the proposed enhancement. - External distinction: Performance and tool demands of tasks/environment relative to present agent capabilities. - Future / prospective distinction: Revised future tool/core behavior, not merely saving a current conversation. - Adaptation option generated: New/modified skills, tools and Rust body code. - Path back into current capability / S3: Accepted promotion changes the running body; Root can roll it back based on current outcome. - Evidence: README self-evolution workflow, agent evolution contract, kernel gate contract, extension authoring. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Shipped self-edit closure is not a claim of measured net capability gain; the README explicitly notes that persistent benefit is not yet demonstrated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Keep ultimate authority over the agent&#39;s identity and non-editable safety/extension charter outside the self-modifying actor. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent efforts to redefine its own authority, protected access and evolution permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and explicitly apply the Root charter defining the owner&#39;s non-negotiable authority envelope. - Decision owner: Human owner; the independent Root validates, signs and enforces the choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner-only charter.toml, charter checks/digest, active charter state, Root sandbox/permission rules and separation from the agent zone. - Closure path: Owner changes charter and invokes charter check --apply → Root validates/activates it → the next runtime decisions are constrained by the new authoritative charter. - Boundary reachability: The installed Root CLI exposes owner-only charter check/apply while the mutable agent is sandboxed away from Root home. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The self-modifying agent does not control the protected charter or Root. Runtime permission requests alone were not counted; the evidenced claim is the explicit higher-level charter authority and return to operation. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether and under which hard non-editable constraints the agent may alter itself, gain access or survive a regression. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human owner only; Root records/enforces the decision. - Return-to-operation path: Active charter affects subsequent agent-zone sandbox and promotion operations. - Evidence: Root charter implementation, owner apply CLI, Root architecture. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Does not infer project-level S5 governance from the existence of maintainers, contributors or release approvals.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1/assessments/in/init-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1\">061d70e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:56:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sora Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness/tree/cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\">cbfddaf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce local tool-mediated task results, translations and project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: input formats, provider/tool errors, partial results, project state and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose operations/tools and revise task work after observed results. - Decision owner: the model-backed main actor and supported child actors in their respective task scopes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentLoop, provider backends, tool registry, workspace restrictions, step/call limits, checkpoints and subprocess execution. - Closure path: tool results append to model history; errors or partial state lead to revised actions, while terminal results are persisted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actors leaves wrappers/storage/guards without discretionary problem solving. - Evidence: desktop/runtime/agent/loop.py; desktop/application/conversations.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/tools/registry.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: ConversationApplication invokes the first-party AgentLoop; the child runner constructs that same application and sends/resumes a message.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent logical and physical write interference between concurrent parent/child operational tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers sharing a project can overwrite the same target or publish partial/inconsistent translation packages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose disjoint work/target-ID ranges and separate result paths, revise overlapping scopes and decide serialized final publication. - Decision owner: the main model actor following the supplied subagent/tool and collaboration mode; locks only enforce exclusive writes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped subagent tools, actual child status/results, send/continue messages, shared mutation-lock propagation and file-lock serialization. - Closure path: the main actor reads actual results, checks missing/duplicate/out-of-range IDs and file contents, corrects directly or sends revised child instructions, then commits the reconciled package serially. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the main actor a lock can serialize two incompatible edits but cannot choose semantically disjoint task boundaries or reconcile their outputs. - Evidence: desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/application/mutationgate.py; desktop/tools/registry.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py; desktop/application/skills.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Optional enabled multi-actor mode, not every single-agent chat. Configured child models/profiles are normal deployment inputs. The lock alone would not justify A. - Distinct S1 units: main task actor and independent-history child translation, review or research task actors with separate outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-workspace writes, conflicting edits to the same target and overlapping or incomplete target-ID coverage. - Attenuating coordination relation: agent-selected non-overlapping ranges/result files, central serial apply/build and corrective scope messages, backed by cross-process write exclusion. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: real child states and artifact coverage checks cause corrected delegation/continuation or revised publication decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party tool instructions and collaboration rules explicitly identify shared-file/logical collisions and complete-ID publication, rather than merely exposing a generic mailbox. - Boundary reachability: enabled subagent profiles bind real tool capabilities in SubagentManager; the built-in tool description supplies coordination rules to the model. The translation plugin&#39;s skill root and SkillService make the concrete disjoint-ID/serial-commit workflow available in the supported product mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No supplied whole-application discretionary current-control function is established beyond bounded task coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child runtime limits, failed work and local task progression. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent status/cancel/continue, per-role step/time/call limits, run records and main-agent task aggregation. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/runs.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material independent complementary audit path is supplied beyond ordinary operational review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete child claims and translation/schema errors are checked in the production workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actual-file reads, acceptance reports, review subtasks, tool receipts and run/checkpoint logs. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md; skills/game-translation/references/candidate-review.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/session.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Independent histories and raw-file availability alone do not turn every review into S3. This is bounded to the supplied product/skill modes, not a prohibition on a custom audit design.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported game layouts, context limits and task-relevant reference information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin/skill loading, retrieval, compaction and project-local adapter authoring. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/custom-adapters.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/application/skills.py; desktop/runtime/context/compaction.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary user task scope, prompt presets, permissions and plugin enablement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt configuration, user questions/steering, per-profile instructions and tool allowlists. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/prompts/config.py; desktop/prompts/assembly.py; desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/tools/contracts.py; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; README.en.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68/assessments/so/sora-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68\">de3f319 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T19:04:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ashishpatel26/Enterprise-Agent-Harness-Project-Template\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Enterprise Agent Harness Template</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ashishpatel26/Enterprise-Agent-Harness-Project-Template/tree/02d40b3cd712af07d5e5ccab12edee9980b988fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02d40b3cd712af07d5e5ccab12edee9980b988fe\">02d40b3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Distinct research, coding and review specialists perform model-selected work for their assigned subtasks; supervisor itself uses a model-directed delegation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Research/document changes, coding outcomes, partial/incorrect drafts, tool responses, task-specific failures and approval delays. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each specialist model chooses its action or answer from a fresh subtask prompt and any available permitted tools; the supervisor model chooses delegate tool calls and final composition. - Decision owner: Configured specialist agent model at each child-run S1 boundary, implemented via the runnable AgentLoop/tool pipeline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-agent YAML model/tool/limits, isolated child contexts, standard tool execution/guards, checkpoints, retries/recovery and explicit RunState outcomes. - Closure path: Parent task → model-chosen delegate call → distinct child model/tool/observation loop → child result/error → parent feedback/next decision. - Boundary reachability: First-party Harness.build installs delegation and validates shipped supervisor YAML&#39;s specialist names; delegate calls createrun/execute, not merely an example external orchestrator. Integration tests exercise parallel independent children with fake LLMs; configured production inference endpoints were not exercised here. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Child models own discretionary task steps; the host executes checks and records state. The supervisor&#39;s choice of subtask does not erase independent specialist S1 agency. - Evidence: loop, runtime wiring, child registry, integration fixture. - Basis: Installed separate autonomous child-run model/tool feedback, with source and test-case corroboration. - Confidence: High for source-distribution reachability; runtime paid-model outcome not independently verified. - Caveats: Child availability and model quality depend on operator configuration; test assertions with FakeLLM prove paths rather than live autonomy quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: A specific installed attenuating channel for inter-S1 operational conflict is not demonstrated, despite real sub-agent delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping specialist tasks, incompatible answers or resource contention could arise, but the source does not encode detection/settlement of reciprocal S1 clashes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supervisor can assign and repeat work, and static registry validates allowed children; no evidence of separate horizontal S1-to-S1 disturbance arbitration or an S2-specific feedback relation. - Decision owner: Supervisor model as current task manager; deterministic roster/permission validation handles routing, not inter-unit conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagents allowlist, parallel delegate calls, child IDs and shared tenant/tools with separate contexts. - Closure path: Parent delegation → child replies → supervisor current control; no distinct conflict signal between S1 children → attenuating settlement → reciprocal changed operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Delegation, tool routing, serial/parallel sequencing and shared budget accounting alone do not establish the VSM S2 function. - Evidence: delegation, supervisor, integration tests. - Basis: Bounded absence review against the strongest multi-agent candidate. - Confidence: Medium-high; positive S3 supervision is not automatically positive S2 conflict attenuation. - Caveats: A future operator policy could require or build explicit inter-specialist conflict arbitration, but no independent pathway is currently closed in the shipped model.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: A live supervisor controls the present specialist-work portfolio for one parent task by selecting subagents, reviewing outcomes and adjusting assignments under a shared task budget. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Diverse subtasks, inadequate specialist output, failed/paused child runs, concurrent execution and aggregate token/cost limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-driven supervisor chooses which allowed specialist gets each subtask (possibly in parallel), sees child answers/errors/pause status, can delegate corrective work, decides when to synthesize the overall answer. - Decision owner: Configured supervisor model; installed delegate tool and harness enforce its chosen child-run requests and report results. The static budget cap is not itself the S3 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervisor.yaml and supervisor@v1 instructions, child roster, depth ceiling, deterministic child IDs for crash recovery, rolled-up child usage/cost, isolated subtask execution and returned status/answer observations. - Closure path: Supervisor task view → delegate choices → child S1 task execution → successful/error/awaiting-approval tool outcome → supervisor&#39;s next model turn and re-delegation/answer choice, with aggregate cost/usage enforced by parent loop. - Whole-system current view: At the declared parent-task recursion, the supervisor sees its approved specialist roster and actual result/error messages from each dispatched child; shared parent budget counts child usage. It does not see all users/tenants or manage an enterprise fleet. - Current-control decision scope: Current assignment, follow-up/reassignment, review sequencing and acceptance/synthesis of the research/coding/review portfolio, not long-term system strategy or ultimate-policy rights. - Boundary reachability: The first-party composition root installs the delegate registry, shipped supervisor config enumerates specialists, prompt explicitly directs re-delegation on bad results, and integration test runs independent children concurrently and returns results/cost to the parent. This is operational first-party S3 for the bounded task organization, not the mere class name Supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model can choose or revise present portfolio assignments after actual child feedback. Deterministic guard/leases/idempotency alone would not qualify; positive mapping depends on the installed decision path plus child autonomy. - Evidence: supervisor instructions, shipped roster, delegate return, tests. - Basis: Evidence-backed installed supervisory current-control closed loop across autonomous child work, sharply scoped to one task organization. - Confidence: Medium-high (decision-path closure proven at source; live provider correctness and conflict handling not tested). - Caveats: A supervisor may simply accept poor outputs; normative quality of its choices is untested and S3 is not S3 independent audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary reality-checking auditor verifies task claims via alternate evidence and closes findings into current management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad research claims or coding changes can be questioned by review specialist or offline eval regression tests, but their installed evidence paths do not make an independent live audit circuit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: review agent has no first-party truth-access tools and receives only a supervisor-authored subtask prompt; audit log hashes observed events without verifying their truth. Eval runner is a separate test/CI workflow, not a returned S3 corrective channel for live work. - Decision owner: Supervisor decides whether to request review and whether to act on it; review model lacks independent access to workspace/reality evidence, offline scorer/CI operator lives outside the task controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional review sub-agent, tamper-evident AuditLog.verify, policy safety guard, offline test expectations and regression baseline comparison. - Closure path: Supervisor-provided material → review critique → supervisor continuation (an ordinary S1 report); or offline test → regression report. Neither creates independent complementary evidence → audit verdict → enforceable S3 correction through an accountable audit role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Separate model identity alone does not imply independent observational access; hash-chain integrity only proves log continuity, and a CI pass is not an independent audit of actual deployment claims. - Evidence: review spec, audit log, evals, delegation. - Basis: Explicit separation of reviewer model, audit-log transport and offline eval against required S3 complementary-independence conditions. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An operator could independently audit via external tools/records; the current review agent may use its learned knowledge but has no distinct first-party reality observation path in its YAML.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct future-environment scanning/adaptation authority governs the whole task organization beyond current user work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New provider availability, changing task types, tool/schema policy, evolving skill capability and future usage forecasts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human operator controls agent YAML/provider/plugin updates; specialist research serves current delegated question rather than strategic capability change. - Decision owner: Developer/tenant for future architecture; supervisor for present delegation only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin extension points, config reload/deployment, memory stores, eval baselines and model/provider retries. - Closure path: Tasks and current tool feedback → user answer or manual version update; no autonomous prospective environmental intelligence → option selection → S3 negotiation/adopted future capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A research sub-agent and memory embeddings may help present tasks, but do not install an organizational future-modeling/strategy actor. - Evidence: architecture, research spec, runtime. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Tool-using agents can discuss the future within individual tasks; that is not a shipped S4 organizational feedback loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity/policy deliberation or legitimate parent-governed identity-level mandate returns to the supervisor-led organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Security permissions, sensitive data, tool use, tenant accountability and human approval requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Policy YAML and operator settings define guards; human reviewer approves/denies individual risky tool calls and may be prohibited from approving their own call. None evidences identity-level policy revision. - Decision owner: External operator/security policy author for static mandates and approval reviewer for individual actions, not a packaged S5 ultimate governance role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SafetyGuard, default policy, ApprovalService, four-eyes switch, audit log, data redaction and tenant API authentication. - Closure path: Policy rule → tool pause → human yes/no → resumed or denied action. This is a closed ordinary permission loop, not identity/mission issue → ultimate authority → revised governance mandate to the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Rigorous approvals and tamper-evident security logging enforce chosen policy, but do not confer first-party S5 or the Methodology&#39;s parent-mode P without a function-specific identity decision right. - Evidence: approvals, guard, policy. - Basis: Bounded absence review distinguishing action authorization from organizational identity-policy closure. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: In a real enterprise, external human governance likely exists; it is not an encoded operational S5 mode within this shipped template.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c962f1ebb771b64d589d68a59cb87abe2ab44f96/assessments/en/enterprise-agent-harness-template.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c962f1ebb771b64d589d68a59cb87abe2ab44f96\">c962f1e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:16:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhanghaojie513/Dougong\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dougong</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhanghaojie513/Dougong/tree/5d7ff991baa0da919c580f418ed5415b50ba3a94\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5d7ff991baa0da919c580f418ed5415b50ba3a94\">5d7ff99 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed problem solving through iterative use of file, search, shell, MCP and delegated task tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown workspace state, model responses, tool errors, user task changes and subprocess results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tools and arguments, delegate self-contained subtasks, interpret results and select the next task action or final response. - Decision owner: Primary and child model-backed agent loops; the host supplies protocols but not the concrete solution steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kernel registration, tool dispatcher, approval policy, JSONL session commit, schema validation, workspace path guard, model connectors, retry and tool-round limits. - Closure path: Prompt/history → IChatClient completion → model-selected tool call → dispatcher execution → tool/child result → subsequent IChatClient turn and final task output. - Boundary reachability: Default SessionComposition registers tools and creates LoopAgentRuntime in both CLI and ACP paths; SubagentTool uses the same runtime class with fresh child history. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model choice leaves permission enforcement and protocol handling, but no substantive next-step/task-selection discretion. - Evidence: LoopAgentRuntime.RunAsync, SessionComposition tool registration, ToolDispatcher. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired model/tool feedback; external provider outcome not executed during this review. - Caveats: Offline probes and advertised provider benchmarks are not used as evidence of real-task completion rates.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate contention between concurrent task-owning sessions or worker calls accessing the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping shell/tool mutation and inconsistent shared-workspace state when independently executing S1 cells interfere; specifically two supported ACP sessions entering exclusive workspace actions. - Distinct S1 units: Two concurrently model-operated ACP sessions in one process, and primary/child independent task loops when their scoped tools overlap. - Inter-S1 disturbance: First-party G9 case explicitly models two session gates entering the same workspace and risking simultaneous exclusive operations; unguarded writes can overlap. - Attenuating coordination relation: Process-shared normalized workspace mutex for serial/exclusive tools; a per-dispatcher resource-key read/write gate for declared tool resources; execution lease delays conflicting calls. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The blocked call executes only after the lease clears, then its concrete tool result reaches its requesting agent&#39;s next model turn. The coordination changes the timing/observable world state of subsequent operations; refusal/error is also returned through dispatcher. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The gate explicitly targets a concrete conflict among autonomous work cells (same workspace competing tool mutations), with conflict-dependent exclusion and resumed tool feedback; it is not merely a queue of tasks or parent-to-child dispatch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Apply or revise the actual inter-session resource-exclusion relation. The supplied resource gate deterministically applies declared plans; no autonomous agent is shipped as an owner of shared conflict-policy discretion. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic constructor/runtime resource gate; developers declare tool concurrency and can compose policy authority. Agent models choose tasks but do not decide lock admissibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SharedWorkspaceLocks, semaphores, stable resource keys, registry limits and ToolExecutionPlan. - Closure path: Competing tool intents → gate classification and lease acquisition → serialized protected execution → result back to the relevant agent; construction of an adaptive autonomous coordination owner is not part of this standard distribution. - Boundary reachability: CLI/ACP SessionComposition constructs a non-isolated ToolResourceGate; the static shared lock table is reached by serial/workspace-exclusive tool invocations, corroborated by pinned G9 probe. Resource-key locks are instance-scoped and are not claimed to protect arbitrary file edits across independent dispatchers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing agents&#39; discretionary policy leaves the same lock decision and resource exclusion; the function-specific path is real, but not an agent-owned S2 closure, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: ToolResourceGate, G9 cross-session counterproof, SubagentTool resource classification, host wiring. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded coordination; not a claim of universal cross-worker file conflict protection. - Caveats: Asynchronously spawned children outlive the spawn-tool lease; cross-process locks and all resource-key paths are not established.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Allocate and revise current task commitments across the primary agent&#39;s active child-agent work portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel worker availability, failed delegated results, conflicting subtask priorities and finite execution capacity during one task composition. - Whole-system current view: Agent-accessible subagentlist exposes all handles in this composition with current running state, and subagentresult returns terminal status, text, error and consumed tool rounds; getgoal and joblist expose current local task/goal state. - Current-control decision scope: Primary model chooses which child work to launch or postpone, which results to retrieve, whether to execute remaining work itself and what follow-up action to assign, within a four-concurrent-child and bounded-depth envelope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commit or revise currently active work distribution based on live child and goal results. - Decision owner: Primary conversational model; SubagentRegistry and goal/dispatcher implement its choices but do not choose which task to prioritize. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child handles, concurrency gate, depth bounds, one-shot result collection, goal state, optional job status and tool permission checks. - Closure path: Primary chooses subtask → asynchronous child run → model requests list/result → receives current status/diagnostic → decides to delegate another task, wait, adjust, work directly or finish in a subsequent loop iteration. - Boundary reachability: SessionComposition registers subagent, subagentlist, subagentresult and goal tools with the standard CLI/ACP model dispatcher; the supplied child loop uses the same IChatClient and shared registry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove primary model discretion and the runtime still enforces slots and reports status but does not decide task allocation, current commitments or recovery. - Evidence: SubagentTools, SubagentRegistry, SessionComposition registration and child runtime. - Basis: structural; agent discretion in the standard tool-call loop is explicit. - Confidence: medium for current-control scope, high for callable feedback path. - Caveats: S3 scope is the one primary-session task portfolio, not a global dashboard controlling all separately instantiated hosts. No parent-governed S3 mode is evidenced by the optional plan approver.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complementary direct-reality audit that reports corrective findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool failure, approval denial and stale session reports are ordinarily visible, but no separate auditor authority samples them independently. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge the operational claim with materially different access and return corrective findings to S3; not wired. - Decision owner: Ordinary dispatcher and logger record their own actions; a human plan approver can accept a proposed plan, not audit completed operational claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: JSONL commit barrier, ToolDispatcher approval logs, user plan review, diagnostics, self-check comparison suite and subagent result summaries. - Closure path: Tool status returns directly to requesting model or is stored for replay; no independent auditor sampling → judgment → corrective returned control loop is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A child can hypothetically be tasked to review a patch, but the default host has no protected independent reviewer role, separate evidence path or mandatory corrective audit closure. - Evidence: JsonlAgentStore, ToolDispatcher, PlanMode, SubagentTools. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: External code reviewers and offline self-check probes are outside the assessed operational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped, operationally closed external-prospective capability adaptation process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/provider changes, evolving user preferences and plugin capability opportunities could merit adaptation, but the default loop addresses current tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluate future-relevant environmental options and authorize capability change returned to current S3/operations; not supplied as a complete first-party chain. - Decision owner: User/developer supplies tool plugins, provider and preferences; models choose available current task actions and can write plans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin lifecycle/hot swap, skill discovery, optional L1 MemoryStore/MemoryInjector, session compaction and manual config. - Closure path: Whitelisted Enabled memory would be injected into later turns, but the shipped CLI has no writer invoking MemoryStore.PutAsync/AdvanceCursorAsync to create, judge, approve and enable new L1 artifacts from outside/future evidence. No prospective option judgment and capability change loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Temporal persistence and dynamic plugin loading do not make a model-owned strategic adaptation decision; optional memory readback is a support surface only. - Evidence: MemoryArtifact, MemoryInjector, SessionComposition, README.en L1 claims. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the supported runtime boundary. - Caveats: The separate experiment proving preference adherence does not establish a deployed preference-learning decision owner; embedded SDK consumers could add the missing cycle independently.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime resolution of organizational identity or ultimate-policy dilemmas with authoritative return into subsequent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dangerous tools, security/privacy switches, permission escalation and plan approval are execution risks within a preselected organizational purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a contested identity-level/ultimate-policy matter and enact the new constitutional constraint in the operating harness; no such first-party path. - Decision owner: User/operator configures permission/environment, human approver authorizes individual tool or plan transitions, and developer chooses fixed policy implementation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SecuritySwitches, PermissionPolicyApprover, approval content binding, restricted L1 preference whitelist and plan review. - Closure path: User-approved actions change current task execution; configuration fixed at startup bounds tool access. No identity/policy proposal → legitimate ultimate authority → governing response → durable changed operation loop exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Approval is local task authorization; neither model nor operator tool prompt resolves identity or ultimate policy of the operating organization. - Evidence: PermissionPolicyApprover, PlanMode, SecuritySwitches via host, MemoryArtifact whitelist. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: Maintainer repository-level governance is outside the deployed runtime organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1ab3c938cffea0f2611e346bfce4afc731b23138/assessments/do/dougong.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1ab3c938cffea0f2611e346bfce4afc731b23138\">1ab3c93 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:53:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amiya-special/Dynaflow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DynaFlow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amiya-special/Dynaflow/tree/7451639d197ef844b4157cf40eadac68e9eba8ca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7451639d197ef844b4157cf40eadac68e9eba8ca\">7451639 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: a model-driven subagent interprets a delegated question subtask, chooses its own supported research/tool calls and produces a substantive answer or structured result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain source material, changing factual evidence, calculations, research/tool response differences and errors in executing an assigned subproblem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how to query configured tools, reason about their actual outputs, retry task-local reasoning and deliver a substantive result to the workflow. - Decision owner: the DynaFlow-managed subagent model invoked through Session.worker and the agent() hook; the independent model API service supplies computation but its unrelated internal policies are not borrowed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflowworker.system.txt role constraints, model-compatible Chat Completions adapter, allowed tool schemas, Python executetool bridge, Docker credential isolation, output schema validation, journal and progress events. - Closure path: main workflow delegates subtask → configured worker model interprets prompt and requests relevant tools → tool/model results return into its message history → final text/StructuredOutput is delivered or explicitly marked dead. - Boundary reachability: src/dynaflow/workflow.py provides a real worker model-call loop and Python host tool execution, while workflowruntime/src/engine/hooks.ts runs the worker and returns validated results to the JavaScript script in supported dynamic/forced modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host cannot perform task-specific research/calculate the final substantive result by parsing the static workflow alone; the model-driven worker makes nontrivial task decisions. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/systemprompts.py, workflowruntime/src/engine/hooks.ts, README mode table. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reachable owned subagent execution, not for actual answer accuracy on unrun providers. - Caveats: model serving and externally configured search APIs are separate providers; a default direct baseline is not the basis for counting multi-agent role ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct lateral inter-S1 disturbance attenuation and return path beyond workflow scheduling/resource constraints is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent requests contend for a configured maximum number of execution slots and tokens, but no independent cross-worker semantic collision or negotiated working relationship is specified in the shipped path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the semaphore grants/reclaims permits, the engine numbers calls, and script dependencies order tasks; these are constructor-authored scheduling controls, not a supported S2 conflict-resolution right over peer S1 relations. - Decision owner: static engine limits and caller-authored workflow script, not an identifiable lateral coordination agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Semaphore.acquire/release, global agent cap, shared budget, Promise.all-style workflow orchestration, journal sequence and cancellation. - Closure path: one call awaits capacity and later proceeds when a permit is released; this closes a bounded scheduler resource constraint without showing disturbance-specific feedback changing separate S1 units&#39; conflicting operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel execution and a concurrency cap do not automatically establish the S2 function; no source-evidenced first-party peer contention arbitration/reconciliation was identified. - Distinct S1 units: the individually prompted subagents called by the generated workflow. - Inter-S1 disturbance: potential competition for capacity or contradictory research outputs, but a distinct implemented peer conflict path is not shown. - Attenuating coordination relation: configured semaphore and script dependency sequencing; neither is evidence of an S2-specific cross-worker conflict feedback loop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a waiting slot delays launch, but no path-specific conflict ruling is carried to an already operating peer to regulate its subsequent activity. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: no S2-specific evidence survives this distinction; hence absence rather than an inflated S2=C score. - Evidence: pinned workflowruntime/src/engine/concurrency.ts, hooks.ts, runWorkflow.ts, src/dynaflow/workflow.py. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high at the frozen shipped boundary. - Caveats: a user-generated Workflow could voluntarily implement specialized peer coordination, but arbitrary user-authored scripts are not a guaranteed first-party function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: the main model dynamically plans, launches, monitors, assesses and modifies the current subagent Workflow to complete the operator&#39;s question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed subagents, incomplete/invalid delivery, insufficient evidence, changing research direction, stalled/aborted work and uncertain selection between direct and delegated execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate task-specific workflow scripts; choose serial/parallel subagent composition and verification stages; call Workflow/TaskOutput/TaskStop; revise or rerun workflow when receipts indicate incomplete or contradicted evidence; decide whether to synthesize the final answer. - Decision owner: first-party-instructed main model in Session.run, in the supported dynamic/forced runtime; deterministic engine/host rules act as subordinate gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: main role prompt, mode/forced policy, tool schemas and asynchronous Session.launch/output, status receipts, journal resume, bounded retries, deadline/cancel paths and host&#39;s valid-cloud-agent finalization predicate. - Closure path: user question and current execution constraints → main model chooses/generates a Workflow → subagents work and send progress/results → TaskOutput/delivery/status returned → main model revises/reruns/stops or completes answer under immutable host acceptance rules. - Boundary reachability: src/dynaflow/assets/workflowmain.system.txt and forcedprompt.txt require the described lead action; src/dynaflow/workflow.py exposes the actual tool calls, stores and returns receipts, and supports re-launch/resume and output blocking. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike the fixed semaphore or outer question queue, the model chooses and changes the task-specific subagent program and how to respond to unsatisfactory execution, giving it a bona fide current whole-workflow management right. - Whole-system current view: active local Workflow invocations, completed run IDs/receipts, failed or valid delivery, current subagent responses and original question acceptance requirements. - Current-control decision scope: question-local orchestration/recovery/answer readiness; not global resource governance over multiple independently funded projects or stable policy change. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/workflow.py (Session.run, launch, output), first-party main/forced prompts, workflowruntime/src/engine/runWorkflow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the supported main-agent-in-the-loop operating mode. - Caveats: actual use of the optional dynamic Workflow is conditional; in direct, no subagent organization exists. The model decides current workflow design, but cannot bypass host tool restriction, budget, delivery validity or user stop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped separately tasked, independently evidenced challenge of an operating agent&#39;s correctness claim with a mandated corrective feedback return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: malformed JSON, empty/invalid deliveries and model outputs lacking required successful-agent provenance are caught, but these are validity/reachability guards, not independent evidence-based challenge of substantive claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: constructor code checks schemas, transport receipts and whether a successful configured subagent delivered results; no distinct substantive reviewer actor is given a protected right to inspect contradicting primary evidence and return adjudicated findings. - Decision owner: deterministic validateAgentDelivery and host finalization predicates for format/provenance; model-driven main agent may inspect results but is also author of the plan and final synthesis, not an independent assessor by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: StructuredOutput JSON Schema, validateDelivery, validcloudagents, TaskOutput, run journal and optional user-created script verification steps. - Closure path: subagent output → schema/empty-result and successful-delivery checks → accepted/rejected receipt or current orchestration re-run; no default complementary channel challenges whether the answer is true against independently obtained source/acceptance evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: syntactic validation is constructor logic, and ad hoc optional model-generated verification does not establish a separate first-party audit mandate or epistemic independence. - Claim being audited: supposed successful and correct response to the original research question. - Ordinary reporting path: subagent task output/status delivered through the host. - Complementary access path: no standard required reviewer with its own independent primary-source acquisition; the main model can read task outputs and optionally design a verification stage. - Independence boundary: no deployed distinct reviewer identity, read-only isolation or mandatory independently sourced cross-check shown. - Who acts on findings: the main agent may rerun after failed delivery, but that is ordinary current-control recovery without an independently warranted challenge verdict. - Evidence: pinned workflowruntime/src/engine/deliveryValidation.ts, runWorkflow.ts, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/assets/workflowmain.system.txt. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specific generated script could use separate verification subagents and alter a future task-specific score, but existence of arbitrary script capability is not evidence of a shipped S3 audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no ongoing first-party environment intelligence function that anticipates changes and modifies the longer-term capabilities/strategy of the DynaFlow work organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel user questions and alternate model research sources are present-work inputs, not demonstrated changes in future capability demand. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses an appropriate Workflow for a single question, and developers may change runtime code, but no bounded S4 agent authorizes prospective operating capability adaptations. - Decision owner: question-local main model for current-work plan and external maintainers/users for updates or future workloads. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamically generated workflow scripts, run records, mode configuration and academic research documentation. - Closure path: incoming question → generated current-work script → result; no current environmental trend → prospective adaptation proposal → installed new future organizational capability loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dynamic per-task program generation is not automatically S4; the generated script executes and is discarded/archived as that task&#39;s procedure, rather than constituting a persistent policy/capability shift. - Evidence: pinned README, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/runner.py, workflowruntime/src/engine/runWorkflow.ts. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human research team may analyze DynaFlow experiments to change later algorithms, outside this assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no internally closed legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision function for a DynaFlow installation is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: API credentials, allowed research tools, direct-versus-workflow policy, Docker capability restrictions, quotas and required delivery are fixed by constructor configuration/user mode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: mode selection and engine constraints come from administrator/configuration; main model cannot rewrite the execution rules or determine ultimate purpose outside the supplied question. - Decision owner: external deployer/evaluation owner and static constructor policy rather than an autonomous organization-level constitutional actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompt, configured tool list, credential isolation, Docker network/privilege restrictions, budget/agent caps and STOPBYUSER marker. - Closure path: externally specified policy → enforced model/tool permissions and delivery constraints; no legitimate identity/ultimate-policy dispute is adjudicated and returned to govern the future organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: obeying a forced mode and enforcing security restrictions are real constraints, but not first-party S5 political/identity decisions. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/configuration.py, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/runner.py, src/dynaflow/assets/forcedprompt.txt. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: high for the narrow supplied runtime. - Caveats: the paper authors and external academic laboratory have policy and strategy outside this executable product; these are not imported as its first-party S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/83a9181e16af7074e956c39286049a23c0cad297/assessments/dy/dynaflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 83a9181e16af7074e956c39286049a23c0cad297\">83a9181 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
